(238643, 13873, 3102, '<td valign="top">Congrats on the win -- I really thought this could be one of the bridges in my area!  We have a lot of ''em that look very much like this one.  Well done!</td>', 'Congrats on the win -- I really thought this could be one of the bridges in my area!  We have a lot of ''em that look very much like this one.  Well done!', '2003-03-17 09:41:40.000000', NULL, 0),
(238652, 13536, 3102, '<td valign="top">Many thanks for all the kind words!  I was rather lucky in this challenge, considering that our area has one of the highest concentrations of bridges in the country, if not the world!<br/> <br/>My brother, a photographer at the Pittsburgh Post-Gazette, shot a spectacular photo of this bridge last fall when they added new lights to the span:<br/> <br/><a href="//www.post-gazette.com/journal/photos_display.asp?ID=8984" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">//www.post-gazette.com/journal/photos_display.asp?ID=8984</a> <br/></td>', 'Many thanks for all the kind words!  I was rather lucky in this challenge, considering that our area has one of the highest concentrations of bridges in the country, if not the world! My brother, a photographer at the Pittsburgh Post-Gazette, shot a spectacular photo of this bridge last fall when they added new lights to the span: //www.post-gazette.com/journal/photos_display.asp?ID=8984 ', '2003-03-17 09:47:47.000000', NULL, 0),
(238703, 13821, 641, '<td valign="top">Thanks everyone for the really helpful comments. It''s *really* appreciated! I now have some ideas in mind, and am ready to drive back out there and try the shot again.</td>', 'Thanks everyone for the really helpful comments. It''s *really* appreciated! I now have some ideas in mind, and am ready to drive back out there and try the shot again.', '2003-03-17 10:24:13.000000', NULL, 0),
(238709, 13873, 641, '<td valign="top">Congratulations! I really loved this one. Very well done!</td>', 'Congratulations! I really loved this one. Very well done!', '2003-03-17 10:27:04.000000', NULL, 0),
(238711, 13536, 641, '<td valign="top">Congratulations....very beautiful shot.</td>', 'Congratulations....very beautiful shot.', '2003-03-17 10:28:17.000000', NULL, 0),
(238712, 13903, 641, '<td valign="top">Like I noted before.. wow! Congratulations on the ribbon.</td>', 'Like I noted before.. wow! Congratulations on the ribbon.', '2003-03-17 10:29:06.000000', NULL, 0),
(238822, 13644, 5266, '<td valign="top">This bridge is no longer in use - the road that used to lead into the bridge is grown up with these twigs/branches so unless I chopped them down (which I didn''t want to do) they''re going to be in the photo.  I tried it in B&amp;W and couldn''t decide which I liked best.  The outtakes are <a href="//www.pbase.com/kiml/bridges" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">here</a></td>', 'This bridge is no longer in use - the road that used to lead into the bridge is grown up with these twigs/branches so unless I chopped them down (which I didn''t want to do) they''re going to be in the photo.  I tried it in B&W and couldn''t decide which I liked best.  The outtakes are here', '2003-03-17 11:22:06.000000', NULL, 0),
(238828, 13551, 5266, '<td valign="top"><b>Probably not going to do very well because it doesn''t show the bridge proper, but the support structure was the most impressive thing that I found to photograph.</b> <br/> <br/>LOL!! 7th Place!! Great showing!!  Love the photo - glad it did so well.</td>', 'Probably not going to do very well because it doesn''t show the bridge proper, but the support structure was the most impressive thing that I found to photograph.  LOL!! 7th Place!! Great showing!!  Love the photo - glad it did so well.', '2003-03-17 11:24:58.000000', NULL, 0),
(238829, 13763, 117, '<td valign="top">Thanks for everyone voting and commments. This is the longest covered single-spaned bridge in the world.</td>', 'Thanks for everyone voting and commments. This is the longest covered single-spaned bridge in the world.', '2003-03-17 11:25:23.000000', NULL, 0),
(238836, 13846, 2752, '<td valign="top">Hi swash. I read the comments and must say that I agree with most of them that although the subjects are lovely ,colors don''t vary enough to have the bridge stand out.<br/>No sense of depth..different time of day would have helped.<a href="https://ngis.ca/~camelotnorth/" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">ngis.ca/~camelotnorth/</a></td>', 'Hi swash. I read the comments and must say that I agree with most of them that although the subjects are lovely ,colors don''t vary enough to have the bridge stand out.No sense of depth..different time of day would have helped.ngis.ca/~camelotnorth/', '2003-03-17 11:33:57.000000', NULL, 0),
(238865, 13746, 4494, '<td valign="top">Surprised this one and the other one didn''t do better, although anything in the 6''s is good (for me anyway).  I really like it since it''s very unique.</td>', 'Surprised this one and the other one didn''t do better, although anything in the 6''s is good (for me anyway).  I really like it since it''s very unique.', '2003-03-17 11:59:59.000000', NULL, 0),
(238886, 13873, 714, '<td valign="top">Great shot!! One of my top 10''s.  Congrats on 1st place. You deserve it!!</td>', 'Great shot!! One of my top 10''s.  Congrats on 1st place. You deserve it!!', '2003-03-17 12:21:44.000000', NULL, 0),
(238905, 13794, 6847, '<td valign="top">Not that I''m saying this isn''t a fantastic picture and I''m sure that allot of people have stood in this location and thought that it would make a great shot but, it does have a very close resemblance to a picture found in this tutorial <a href="//www3.photosig.com/viewarticle.php?id=671" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">//www3.photosig.com/viewarticle.php?id=671</a></td>', 'Not that I''m saying this isn''t a fantastic picture and I''m sure that allot of people have stood in this location and thought that it would make a great shot but, it does have a very close resemblance to a picture found in this tutorial //www3.photosig.com/viewarticle.php?id=671', '2003-03-17 12:32:26.000000', NULL, 0),
(238957, 13846, 1005, '<td valign="top">Maybe I should have titled this "Waldo''s Bridge" or "Bridge In Harmony With Nature"......<br/> <br/>A less compressed, nicer version of this photo is located at:<br/><a href="//www.deviantart.com/deviation/1473762" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">//www.deviantart.com/deviation/1473762</a> <br/>But honestly, it''s not all that different.<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2003-03-18 12:04:28.</i></td>', 'Maybe I should have titled this "Waldo''s Bridge" or "Bridge In Harmony With Nature"...... A less compressed, nicer version of this photo is located at://www.deviantart.com/deviation/1473762 But honestly, it''s not all that different.', '2003-03-17 12:54:17.000000', '2003-03-18 12:04:28.000000', 0),
(238983, 13930, 2542, '<td valign="top">Thank all of you for taking the time and commenting on my picture ... I appreciate it<br/> <br/>cheers,<br/>Vilborg</td>', 'Thank all of you for taking the time and commenting on my picture ... I appreciate it cheers,Vilborg', '2003-03-17 13:12:19.000000', NULL, 0),
(238989, 13889, 2829, '<td valign="top">A visit from the Critique Club ;)<br/> <br/>I like this image, but I think the emphasis here is on the building more than the bridge. I think you should make it stand out more. Different angle and point of view maybe? Even different lighting to strengthen the texture. <br/> <br/>I''m not sure if you oversharpened this photo but I can see some jaggies in diagonal lines. I''m neutral on the colors they don''t seem to be oversaturated but they have enough contrast to balance this photo out. Too bad about the shadows on the bridge.<br/> <br/>I like how the photo displays the age of this bridge. And a nice sleek frame. A good contender but I think you could improve on this by using a  different angle.</td>', 'A visit from the Critique Club ;) I like this image, but I think the emphasis here is on the building more than the bridge. I think you should make it stand out more. Different angle and point of view maybe? Even different lighting to strengthen the texture.  I''m not sure if you oversharpened this photo but I can see some jaggies in diagonal lines. I''m neutral on the colors they don''t seem to be oversaturated but they have enough contrast to balance this photo out. Too bad about the shadows on the bridge. I like how the photo displays the age of this bridge. And a nice sleek frame. A good contender but I think you could improve on this by using a  different angle.', '2003-03-17 13:18:03.000000', NULL, 0),
(239024, 13794, 3032, '<td valign="top">Maaaaannnnnnnnnnn! Just missed the ribbon! :(<br/>Congrats anyway, good photo.</td>', 'Maaaaannnnnnnnnnn! Just missed the ribbon! :(Congrats anyway, good photo.', '2003-03-17 13:45:13.000000', NULL, 0),
(239069, 13873, 2667, '<td valign="top">This a wonderfully evocative image with an outstanding tonal range. It was a tough week with lots of great competition and this makes for a well deserved win. Congratulations!</td>', 'This a wonderfully evocative image with an outstanding tonal range. It was a tough week with lots of great competition and this makes for a well deserved win. Congratulations!', '2003-03-17 14:50:23.000000', NULL, 0),
(239126, 13882, 6806, '<td valign="top">Thank all of you so much for the comments on my picture of the bridge. This was the first time that I submitted a picture. Looking forward to more help. Shirley<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2003-03-17 15:47:17.</i></td>', 'Thank all of you so much for the comments on my picture of the bridge. This was the first time that I submitted a picture. Looking forward to more help. Shirley', '2003-03-17 15:45:19.000000', '2003-03-17 15:47:17.000000', 0),
(239155, 13977, 1054, '<td valign="top">*Critique Club* <br/> <br/>FIRST IMPRESSION: Great sweeping curves. I love the texture, light and shadow.<br/> <br/>CHALLENGE: Meets the challenge.<br/> <br/>COMPOSITION: Excellent composition. The lines of the bridge cause the viewer''s eye to sweep across the photograph, taking in all the detail.<br/> <br/>TECHNICAL: Great exposure, good detail from light to dark with no blowouts.  The use of black and white makes the focus of this shot the bridge and its texture/light/shadows, rather than the brighter leaves of the trees. The sweeping curves and light through the bridge railings really set this image apart from the others.<br/> <br/>CONCLUSION: A great image, one of my top scorers this week.<br/> <br/>Thanks for sharing and good luck in future challenges!</td>', '*Critique Club*  FIRST IMPRESSION: Great sweeping curves. I love the texture, light and shadow. CHALLENGE: Meets the challenge. COMPOSITION: Excellent composition. The lines of the bridge cause the viewer''s eye to sweep across the photograph, taking in all the detail. TECHNICAL: Great exposure, good detail from light to dark with no blowouts.  The use of black and white makes the focus of this shot the bridge and its texture/light/shadows, rather than the brighter leaves of the trees. The sweeping curves and light through the bridge railings really set this image apart from the others. CONCLUSION: A great image, one of my top scorers this week. Thanks for sharing and good luck in future challenges!', '2003-03-17 16:04:54.000000', NULL, 0),
(239333, 13641, 554, '<td valign="top">eue wuz robed</td>', 'eue wuz robed', '2003-03-17 18:03:17.000000', NULL, 0),
(239334, 13641, 554, '<td valign="top">eue wuz robed ...... I gave it a 10</td>', 'eue wuz robed ...... I gave it a 10', '2003-03-17 18:03:51.000000', NULL, 0),
(239348, 13873, 3575, '<td valign="top">Wonderful, wonderful photograph. Congratulations on your enviable blue ribbon!</td>', 'Wonderful, wonderful photograph. Congratulations on your enviable blue ribbon!', '2003-03-17 18:17:28.000000', NULL, 0),
(239392, 13793, 3942, '<td valign="top">I never expected such a high score for this shot and I think, this one doesn''t deserve it. But nevertheless thank you very much for your highly interesting comments. A lot of them are completely oppositional. Some of you like the colored umbrella, others would like the shot better without it. My first choice for this challenge was this shot: <a href="//www.200bar.de/nds/show_pic.php?id=111" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">//www.200bar.de/nds/show_pic.php?id=111</a> . Unfortunately I didn''t achieve the desired effect without without spot editing. So I had to submit this IMO a little poor photo...</td>', 'I never expected such a high score for this shot and I think, this one doesn''t deserve it. But nevertheless thank you very much for your highly interesting comments. A lot of them are completely oppositional. Some of you like the colored umbrella, others would like the shot better without it. My first choice for this challenge was this shot: //www.200bar.de/nds/show_pic.php?id=111 . Unfortunately I didn''t achieve the desired effect without without spot editing. So I had to submit this IMO a little poor photo...', '2003-03-17 19:12:46.000000', NULL, 0),
(239431, 13575, 5865, '<td valign="top">Thanks for the encouragement Pat and Sonja.  Maybe I will manage to come last for the next submission :)  Good luck with yours.<br/> <br/>Anthony.</td>', 'Thanks for the encouragement Pat and Sonja.  Maybe I will manage to come last for the next submission :)  Good luck with yours. Anthony.', '2003-03-17 20:02:11.000000', NULL, 0),
(239444, 13648, 6536, '<td valign="top"><br/> <br/>Nice shot. Very creative. Good cropping<br/><br/><i>Message edited by HBunch - Crituque Club Status Removed.</i></td>', ' Nice shot. Very creative. Good croppingMessage edited by HBunch - Crituque Club Status Removed.', '2003-03-17 20:36:04.000000', NULL, 0),
(239452, 13778, 6536, '<td valign="top"><br/> <br/>Nice angle. Good cropping. Nice job.<br/><br/><i>Message edited by HBunch - Crituque Club Status Removed.</i></td>', ' Nice angle. Good cropping. Nice job.Message edited by HBunch - Crituque Club Status Removed.', '2003-03-17 20:39:07.000000', NULL, 0),
(239453, 13791, 6536, '<td valign="top"><br/> <br/>I like this shot. Great angle. Nice cropping &amp; contrast. Great job.<br/><br/><i>Message edited by HBunch - Crituque Club Status Removed.</i></td>', ' I like this shot. Great angle. Nice cropping & contrast. Great job.Message edited by HBunch - Crituque Club Status Removed.', '2003-03-17 20:40:38.000000', NULL, 0);
INSERT INTO comments ("id", "image_id", "commenter_id", "raw_comment", "comment", "date", "edited", "made_during_challenge") VALUES
(239456, 13532, 554, '<td valign="top">Ed, at first look this photo did not interest me much because it is so dark. The shadows...left, foreground and right looked very un-natural to me and after reading your comments I still do not understand how they look the way they do.<br/>The framing is perfect and takes one''s eye directly to the subject(and beyond). Without the title you would wonder what was going on here but using titles is very much a part of photography and you named it well.<br/>The overall colors and tones work really well together. You tuned it beautifully. Dark photos usually do not place well on dpc and to settle on #67 out of 229 you did EXTREMELY well.<br/>You know, after really LOOKING at your picture, dark really works. The light colored building on the left adds to the relaxing feeling I get while enjoying this very nice shot.</td>', 'Ed, at first look this photo did not interest me much because it is so dark. The shadows...left, foreground and right looked very un-natural to me and after reading your comments I still do not understand how they look the way they do.The framing is perfect and takes one''s eye directly to the subject(and beyond). Without the title you would wonder what was going on here but using titles is very much a part of photography and you named it well.The overall colors and tones work really well together. You tuned it beautifully. Dark photos usually do not place well on dpc and to settle on #67 out of 229 you did EXTREMELY well.You know, after really LOOKING at your picture, dark really works. The light colored building on the left adds to the relaxing feeling I get while enjoying this very nice shot.', '2003-03-17 20:59:58.000000', NULL, 0),
(239539, 13573, 4811, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>COMPOSITION... This is a powerfully composed shot, with the horizon line along the bottom third of the image, the bridge forming a path for your eye to follow, from one corner of the photo all the way to the other, to the simpleness and clarity of the rest of the shop. All major elements of a strong composition exist: leading lines, rule of thirds, getting close to the subject, keeping the rest simple...  What''s distracting in the composition is the electrical pylon, and to a much lesser extent the lights on the bridge. I could live with the streetlights - they are evenly spaced and don''t really interfere with the image that much but the pylon just distracts the viewer a little too much. A small change in angle would also have eliminated seeing the headlights from the other side of the bridge, leaving fewer of them in the image and creating in my opinion, a more powerful shot. Not much can be done about the pylon short of cloning it out or finding a different viewpoint, but everything else about this angle is appealing ... I would have hated having to try something else just because a pylon happened to be lying about. Perhaps a small shift in angle may have hid most of it behind columns but I doubt that could have helped much...<br/> <br/>TECHNIQUE... What meticulous timing! The sunset has rendered a the sky a dramatic shade of blue, given quite a nice glow to the clouds, and the flare from the sun, not to mention the sun''s placement itself. really gives the shot that extra something :)   The bridge is nicely silhouetted as well.<br/> <br/>OVERALL... A simply beautiful shot! Really wish less of the light posts and pylon were around there, but an excellent shot regardless :)</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club! COMPOSITION... This is a powerfully composed shot, with the horizon line along the bottom third of the image, the bridge forming a path for your eye to follow, from one corner of the photo all the way to the other, to the simpleness and clarity of the rest of the shop. All major elements of a strong composition exist: leading lines, rule of thirds, getting close to the subject, keeping the rest simple...  What''s distracting in the composition is the electrical pylon, and to a much lesser extent the lights on the bridge. I could live with the streetlights - they are evenly spaced and don''t really interfere with the image that much but the pylon just distracts the viewer a little too much. A small change in angle would also have eliminated seeing the headlights from the other side of the bridge, leaving fewer of them in the image and creating in my opinion, a more powerful shot. Not much can be done about the pylon short of cloning it out or finding a different viewpoint, but everything else about this angle is appealing ... I would have hated having to try something else just because a pylon happened to be lying about. Perhaps a small shift in angle may have hid most of it behind columns but I doubt that could have helped much... TECHNIQUE... What meticulous timing! The sunset has rendered a the sky a dramatic shade of blue, given quite a nice glow to the clouds, and the flare from the sun, not to mention the sun''s placement itself. really gives the shot that extra something :)   The bridge is nicely silhouetted as well. OVERALL... A simply beautiful shot! Really wish less of the light posts and pylon were around there, but an excellent shot regardless :)', '2003-03-17 22:34:37.000000', NULL, 0),
(239727, 13565, 1863, '<td valign="top">Wow, each of your pictures gets better and better. I''m impressed, you are certainly improving. Congratulations. :)</td>', 'Wow, each of your pictures gets better and better. I''m impressed, you are certainly improving. Congratulations. :)', '2003-03-18 00:56:26.000000', NULL, 0),
(239730, 13674, 2391, '<td valign="top">I have to say, this is one of the prettiest pictures I''ve seen.  It has such a nice feel to it.</td>', 'I have to say, this is one of the prettiest pictures I''ve seen.  It has such a nice feel to it.', '2003-03-18 00:57:45.000000', NULL, 0),
(239774, 13558, 5792, '<td valign="top">Very nice, original bridge shot. Creativity is the photographer''s best friend. B&amp;W is perfect for this. Once again you have demonstrated your talent at capturing meaningful images.</td>', 'Very nice, original bridge shot. Creativity is the photographer''s best friend. B&W is perfect for this. Once again you have demonstrated your talent at capturing meaningful images.', '2003-03-18 01:29:45.000000', NULL, 0),
(239784, 13939, 6172, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the C.C.!<br/>  First, I really like what you are trying with this photo, I like your willingness to try something different.  I do have to agree with those that say the plant is distracting, but that is not necessarily a bad thing.  The photo contains a bridge, but does not necessarily have to be only about a bridge.  The plant does make the photo more colorfull and interesting.<br/>  Good color and lighting in the photo.  I might have been better if the bridge were just a little lower in the shot.  Some said the bridge was out of focus, I don''t see that, the bridge is just farther away than the plant, so it seems out of focus.  Overall, nice photo.  You probably didn''t have the nice bridges to work with like some do (The ones who medaled this competition.).  So, you had to improvise with what you had.  Keep up the good work!</td>', 'Greetings from the C.C.!  First, I really like what you are trying with this photo, I like your willingness to try something different.  I do have to agree with those that say the plant is distracting, but that is not necessarily a bad thing.  The photo contains a bridge, but does not necessarily have to be only about a bridge.  The plant does make the photo more colorfull and interesting.  Good color and lighting in the photo.  I might have been better if the bridge were just a little lower in the shot.  Some said the bridge was out of focus, I don''t see that, the bridge is just farther away than the plant, so it seems out of focus.  Overall, nice photo.  You probably didn''t have the nice bridges to work with like some do (The ones who medaled this competition.).  So, you had to improvise with what you had.  Keep up the good work!', '2003-03-18 01:37:10.000000', NULL, 0),
(239817, 13873, 1717, '<td valign="top">thank you all again for the kind words and support.  it means so much to me!<br/> <br/>take care...<br/>sher :)</td>', 'thank you all again for the kind words and support.  it means so much to me! take care...sher :)', '2003-03-18 02:50:47.000000', NULL, 0),
(239823, 13818, 4136, '<td valign="top">Thank you to everyone who voted and commented. I am very pleased to have come in 31st of 229. :)<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2003-03-19 01:16:53.</i></td>', 'Thank you to everyone who voted and commented. I am very pleased to have come in 31st of 229. :)', '2003-03-18 03:18:33.000000', '2003-03-19 01:16:53.000000', 0),
(240048, 13903, 806, '<td valign="top">This is an awesome shot.. Had to return to it again today.<br/></td>', 'This is an awesome shot.. Had to return to it again today.', '2003-03-18 09:34:16.000000', NULL, 0),
(240077, 13794, 5728, '<td valign="top">Thanx Az! It was close.<br/> <br/>Dr. Nick, thanx for the link to the tutorial. Next time I''ll know how to do it better I guess.</td>', 'Thanx Az! It was close. Dr. Nick, thanx for the link to the tutorial. Next time I''ll know how to do it better I guess.', '2003-03-18 10:04:02.000000', NULL, 0),
(240145, 13532, 6999, '<td valign="top">Thanks for that comment, David. The darkness? Well, firstly it was a dark day, and secondly a near-nightmare to get the shot, especially with the lack of control settings on the 2800Z. I like the way the dark surroundings emphasise the patch of brightness on the horizon, the way the darkness conceals some of the messiness of the nearby buildings (mostly still under construction), and along with the reflections in the water serve to isolate the bridge: I could almost ahve titled the shot loneliness, but thought that it needed the help of a little information. I also hoped the highlights on the handrails of the bridge (had to sharpen to get them out that way) would give enough of a clue as to the subject - evidently not a successful strategy for most viewers.<br/> <br/>Thanks to all who commented, and of course especially to the five of you responsible for those 10''s!<br/> <br/>Ed</td>', 'Thanks for that comment, David. The darkness? Well, firstly it was a dark day, and secondly a near-nightmare to get the shot, especially with the lack of control settings on the 2800Z. I like the way the dark surroundings emphasise the patch of brightness on the horizon, the way the darkness conceals some of the messiness of the nearby buildings (mostly still under construction), and along with the reflections in the water serve to isolate the bridge: I could almost ahve titled the shot loneliness, but thought that it needed the help of a little information. I also hoped the highlights on the handrails of the bridge (had to sharpen to get them out that way) would give enough of a clue as to the subject - evidently not a successful strategy for most viewers. Thanks to all who commented, and of course especially to the five of you responsible for those 10''s! Ed', '2003-03-18 10:40:33.000000', NULL, 0),
(240237, 13903, 1041, '<td valign="top"><b>A Comment From The Critique Club</b> <br/> <br/>Hi Brian!<br/> <br/>First of all, congratulations on your ribbon, it is well deserved, you submitted a spectaculare photo to the challenge.  It''ll be a pleasure to write a review, but I''m not sure how much I can suggest for improvements ... but I know that I''m tempted to go out and buy a ticket to San Francisco and finally see this bridge with my own eyes!<br/> <br/>Your photo has a very dreamy/mystical feel to it because of the fog.  This is enhanced by your choice of b&amp;w and the nice use of negative space in the left of the picture.  I like how the top of the bridge and the far end of the bridge simply melt into the fog, but the front is very clearly visible, and you can see all those ''little'' cars.<br/> <br/>Your photo is nice and crisp where the bridge isn''t obscured by the fog, and you achieved a nice tonal range.  The frame very nicely complements the picture.<br/> <br/>The photo obviously meets the challenge.  I hope it has a place on your wall somewhere because it deserves it.  Keep up your wonderful work, I checked your portfolio and you have some incredible shots in there :)<br/> <br/>Please let me know if you have any questions or comments about this review.<br/> <br/>Franziska.</td>', 'A Comment From The Critique Club  Hi Brian! First of all, congratulations on your ribbon, it is well deserved, you submitted a spectaculare photo to the challenge.  It''ll be a pleasure to write a review, but I''m not sure how much I can suggest for improvements ... but I know that I''m tempted to go out and buy a ticket to San Francisco and finally see this bridge with my own eyes! Your photo has a very dreamy/mystical feel to it because of the fog.  This is enhanced by your choice of b&w and the nice use of negative space in the left of the picture.  I like how the top of the bridge and the far end of the bridge simply melt into the fog, but the front is very clearly visible, and you can see all those ''little'' cars. Your photo is nice and crisp where the bridge isn''t obscured by the fog, and you achieved a nice tonal range.  The frame very nicely complements the picture. The photo obviously meets the challenge.  I hope it has a place on your wall somewhere because it deserves it.  Keep up your wonderful work, I checked your portfolio and you have some incredible shots in there :) Please let me know if you have any questions or comments about this review. Franziska.', '2003-03-18 11:43:05.000000', NULL, 0),
(240524, 13685, 4493, '<td valign="top">Critique Club Comment:<br/> <br/>This is a nice looking bridge. I think that you have take a wider shot then needed. I think that with the lines in your surroundings the bridge itself gets lost. With the conversion to black and white, you have furthered the bridge being lost. The angle isn''t bad, but with your current crop my eye is drawn to the stop sign and not the bridge. I would like to see a crop of just the width of the bridge. This would minimize what other objects my eyes are drawn to. Also, maybe by keeping the photo in color, we can see more of the detail in the bridge as it would surely be a different color then the background trees. <br/> <br/>Moving in on your subject can sometimes produce great results. Don''t be afraid to try something more artsy with your angles and crops. You may be surprised that an otherwise ordinary snapshot can become a piece of art.</td>', 'Critique Club Comment: This is a nice looking bridge. I think that you have take a wider shot then needed. I think that with the lines in your surroundings the bridge itself gets lost. With the conversion to black and white, you have furthered the bridge being lost. The angle isn''t bad, but with your current crop my eye is drawn to the stop sign and not the bridge. I would like to see a crop of just the width of the bridge. This would minimize what other objects my eyes are drawn to. Also, maybe by keeping the photo in color, we can see more of the detail in the bridge as it would surely be a different color then the background trees.  Moving in on your subject can sometimes produce great results. Don''t be afraid to try something more artsy with your angles and crops. You may be surprised that an otherwise ordinary snapshot can become a piece of art.', '2003-03-18 15:11:48.000000', NULL, 0),
(240565, 13765, 327, '<td valign="top"><b>CC</b> Greetings wackybill  <br/>Antietam- September 17, 1862 That was a long time ago and has an interesting history. Thanks for the information.<br/>Fits The Challenge-Yes <br/>Composition-Nice _but_ <br/>Background-Lots of tree branches. <br/>My Opinion-The composition could of been changed ...just a bit. Like the comment below I think if you had moved down the bank just a bit your shot would of scored higher. HA! I know easy for me to say..I wasn''t there. I think all the tree branches along with the snow make this look slightly over exposed. The shadows of the trees on the stone is very attractive to my eye. Wonderful capture of history.</td>', 'CC Greetings wackybill  Antietam- September 17, 1862 That was a long time ago and has an interesting history. Thanks for the information.Fits The Challenge-Yes Composition-Nice _but_ Background-Lots of tree branches. My Opinion-The composition could of been changed ...just a bit. Like the comment below I think if you had moved down the bank just a bit your shot would of scored higher. HA! I know easy for me to say..I wasn''t there. I think all the tree branches along with the snow make this look slightly over exposed. The shadows of the trees on the stone is very attractive to my eye. Wonderful capture of history.', '2003-03-18 16:08:34.000000', NULL, 0),
(240576, 13911, 327, '<td valign="top"><b>CC</b> <br/>Hey DougPaz <br/>Fits The Challenge-YES <br/>Composition-patterns and shadows <br/>Background-Lovely blue<br/>My Opinion-This is a good photo of a bridge in progress. The sky, light and patterns of shadows are really interesting to look at. Beautiful to my eye. I think the crane adds to the over all  shot, but the crop is too tight at the top. Other then that little nit pick......it''s a good photo and was under rated.<br/></td>', 'CC Hey DougPaz Fits The Challenge-YES Composition-patterns and shadows Background-Lovely blueMy Opinion-This is a good photo of a bridge in progress. The sky, light and patterns of shadows are really interesting to look at. Beautiful to my eye. I think the crane adds to the over all  shot, but the crop is too tight at the top. Other then that little nit pick......it''s a good photo and was under rated.', '2003-03-18 16:21:11.000000', NULL, 0),
(240583, 13960, 327, '<td valign="top"><b>CC</b> <br/>Hi ClubJuggle<br/> <br/>Fits The Challenge-Yes<br/>Composition- See below<br/>Background- Busy<br/>My Opinion- I''m not too sure about this shot and during the challenge rated it a 7 for the same reason. Let me try to explain: I like it, it does look like a painting and technically I think it''s good. The trouble area is the white to right-makes my eye fly there. Then I have trouble finding my bearings..it''s a busy shot. I think if the horizon/sky/or bank had been visable it would of helped this shot. Just MHO.<br/> <br/></td>', 'CC Hi ClubJuggle Fits The Challenge-YesComposition- See belowBackground- BusyMy Opinion- I''m not too sure about this shot and during the challenge rated it a 7 for the same reason. Let me try to explain: I like it, it does look like a painting and technically I think it''s good. The trouble area is the white to right-makes my eye fly there. Then I have trouble finding my bearings..it''s a busy shot. I think if the horizon/sky/or bank had been visable it would of helped this shot. Just MHO. ', '2003-03-18 16:29:34.000000', NULL, 0),
(240621, 13536, 3575, '<td valign="top">Congratulations Alan! This is absolutely gorgeous. It''s beautifully sharp and clean and I like the angle from which you took the photo. Is the water icy? Someday I would really like to know who the gremlins are that score these magnificient works 2, 3, 4 and, in this case, I 5? What are they thinking!</td>', 'Congratulations Alan! This is absolutely gorgeous. It''s beautifully sharp and clean and I like the angle from which you took the photo. Is the water icy? Someday I would really like to know who the gremlins are that score these magnificient works 2, 3, 4 and, in this case, I 5? What are they thinking!', '2003-03-18 18:07:52.000000', NULL, 0),
(240859, 13536, 6753, '<td valign="top">Great shot and exposure. I was remiss in getting any submissions in on time. Shame on me since I live and drive around Cleveland almost daily with our abundance of bridges ala Pittsburgh: Railway, high-level scissor, swing-type etc. Too bad the Bucs aren''t coming to Cleve. for any inter-league play this year, be plenty of seats available at Jacobs<br/>Field and we''re nicer than Brown''s fans. Incidentally , my wife''s maiden name is Freed and you already know I''m sure that a certain Alan Freed is honored right here in the Rock Hall of Fame. Again, great pic.</td>', 'Great shot and exposure. I was remiss in getting any submissions in on time. Shame on me since I live and drive around Cleveland almost daily with our abundance of bridges ala Pittsburgh: Railway, high-level scissor, swing-type etc. Too bad the Bucs aren''t coming to Cleve. for any inter-league play this year, be plenty of seats available at JacobsField and we''re nicer than Brown''s fans. Incidentally , my wife''s maiden name is Freed and you already know I''m sure that a certain Alan Freed is honored right here in the Rock Hall of Fame. Again, great pic.', '2003-03-18 21:47:09.000000', NULL, 0),
(240881, 13554, 6378, '<td valign="top">Yes, I shot it from a moving train.</td>', 'Yes, I shot it from a moving train.', '2003-03-18 22:12:45.000000', NULL, 0),
(240959, 13536, 1717, '<td valign="top">congratulations on a truly beautiful photo!</td>', 'congratulations on a truly beautiful photo!', '2003-03-18 23:42:27.000000', NULL, 0),
(240961, 13903, 1717, '<td valign="top">congratulations on a great shot!!</td>', 'congratulations on a great shot!!', '2003-03-18 23:43:14.000000', NULL, 0),
(240973, 13794, 1717, '<td valign="top">awwwwww...sooooo close!!!  you should be proud of this...it''s wonderful!!!</td>', 'awwwwww...sooooo close!!!  you should be proud of this...it''s wonderful!!!', '2003-03-18 23:54:38.000000', NULL, 0),
(240987, 13710, 674, '<td valign="top">Greetings From the Critique Club<br/>Composition: I like the way that you have cropped this,although it gave me vertigo! I hope you were not really so close to the edge!<br/> Exposure good<br/>Desaturating all but the red channels has given the image a sense of oldness and having withstood the ravages of time!<br/>To quote Dustin "good job". Keep it up.<br/></td>', 'Greetings From the Critique ClubComposition: I like the way that you have cropped this,although it gave me vertigo! I hope you were not really so close to the edge! Exposure goodDesaturating all but the red channels has given the image a sense of oldness and having withstood the ravages of time!To quote Dustin "good job". Keep it up.', '2003-03-19 00:01:41.000000', NULL, 0),
(240999, 13852, 674, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club<br/>Your composition is good nicely framed and tells a story, but it is slightly out of focus. <br/>Colours are a bit soft but overall pleasing with the blues on father and son coming out very well!<br/>Your perspective is very good giving the impression of a very long walk for father and son! you have also captured a good amount of detail both in the light and shadows.<br/>Good luck with your next challenge.</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique ClubYour composition is good nicely framed and tells a story, but it is slightly out of focus. Colours are a bit soft but overall pleasing with the blues on father and son coming out very well!Your perspective is very good giving the impression of a very long walk for father and son! you have also captured a good amount of detail both in the light and shadows.Good luck with your next challenge.', '2003-03-19 00:08:45.000000', NULL, 0),
(241087, 13663, 4007, '<td valign="top">I''m impressed by the fact that I think you managed to push every score lowering button that dpc is infamous for.  You''ve got a soft focus, high grain, blatent nudity, and a stretched interpretation of the challenge.  With that considered I''d say beating out 3 other people is a pretty good finish. =P  In all seriousness though, I do like the colors of this shot and I think it walks an interesting line between being abstract and straight forward.  I''m not sure what I would have scored it (I didn''t vote), but as you said, it''s an experiment, and I''m of the opinion that almost any experimentation is a good thing, this shot includes.  Good luck on future challenges.  Don''t ditch edginess for your scores if you can help it.</td>', 'I''m impressed by the fact that I think you managed to push every score lowering button that dpc is infamous for.  You''ve got a soft focus, high grain, blatent nudity, and a stretched interpretation of the challenge.  With that considered I''d say beating out 3 other people is a pretty good finish. =P  In all seriousness though, I do like the colors of this shot and I think it walks an interesting line between being abstract and straight forward.  I''m not sure what I would have scored it (I didn''t vote), but as you said, it''s an experiment, and I''m of the opinion that almost any experimentation is a good thing, this shot includes.  Good luck on future challenges.  Don''t ditch edginess for your scores if you can help it.', '2003-03-19 01:24:12.000000', NULL, 0),
(241162, 13565, 4494, '<td valign="top">Thanks Annida, I appreciate the compliment!  I''m really "proud" of this picture since it''s my best so far and I hope I can improve as time goes by.  BTW, I really like your pictures also!</td>', 'Thanks Annida, I appreciate the compliment!  I''m really "proud" of this picture since it''s my best so far and I hope I can improve as time goes by.  BTW, I really like your pictures also!', '2003-03-19 02:32:37.000000', NULL, 0),
(241188, 13912, 1863, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>Now, it makes me wonder sometimes why people ask for a critique when lol, you don''t need it!<br/>I can''t think of much that you could have done differently. Maybe if you''d gone further down to the right to get more in, but you really didn''t need to.<br/>The light is perfect. I love how the middle arch seems to glow in the sunlight. The little lights above the bridge seem to draw your eyes further into the photograph. That reflection of the light on the water is exquisite, it looks as if you can walk on it. <br/>The architecture of the bridge is lovely too. It looks like a weird craft, the way the supports are made to look like boats carrying the weight of the bridge. It is all captured here magnificently.<br/>The only thing I would have done to change it?<br/>I would have cropped out the shaded bit on the right of the photo. It takes out a tiny bit, like half an inch of the photo, but, then none of the bridge would be in the shadow. I just love it.<br/>Well done. The score also reflects that a lot of people loved it.<br/>Great job<br/>Annida</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club! Now, it makes me wonder sometimes why people ask for a critique when lol, you don''t need it!I can''t think of much that you could have done differently. Maybe if you''d gone further down to the right to get more in, but you really didn''t need to.The light is perfect. I love how the middle arch seems to glow in the sunlight. The little lights above the bridge seem to draw your eyes further into the photograph. That reflection of the light on the water is exquisite, it looks as if you can walk on it. The architecture of the bridge is lovely too. It looks like a weird craft, the way the supports are made to look like boats carrying the weight of the bridge. It is all captured here magnificently.The only thing I would have done to change it?I would have cropped out the shaded bit on the right of the photo. It takes out a tiny bit, like half an inch of the photo, but, then none of the bridge would be in the shadow. I just love it.Well done. The score also reflects that a lot of people loved it.Great jobAnnida', '2003-03-19 04:17:30.000000', NULL, 0),
(241195, 13822, 3280, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings from the Critique Club };-) </b> <br/> <br/><b>Initial thoughts </b> <br/>Interesting composition and bridge,definitely meets the challenge, a touch off center.<br/> <br/><b>Composition/ Content </b> <br/>Very nice capture of a highly unusual subject. I looked at this quite a while during voting trying to figure out exactly what the story was with this bridge. Never did figure it really. A good eye to find this. B &amp; W works well but I would like to see what this "Tridge" looks like against a blue sky.<br/> <br/><b>Technical</b> <br/>Focus seems good and contrast is excellent for the difference in light and shadow you have here.  The background seems a bit busy but is beyond your control. The shot is just barely set up to the right a little. You may have been able to control this when cropping, but for this shot, it is pretty important. Processing seems good.<br/> <br/><b>My Opinion On The Photo </b> <br/>I originally scored this shot a seven. I think that it meets the challenge well but could have been improved a just little to finish even higher than it did. Good job, and keep up the good work. <br/> <br/> <br/>I would be happy to talk further about this shot if you would like to contact me.<br/><b>DougPaz</b> <br/> <br/></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club };-)   Initial thoughts  Interesting composition and bridge,definitely meets the challenge, a touch off center. Composition/ Content  Very nice capture of a highly unusual subject. I looked at this quite a while during voting trying to figure out exactly what the story was with this bridge. Never did figure it really. A good eye to find this. B & W works well but I would like to see what this "Tridge" looks like against a blue sky. Technical Focus seems good and contrast is excellent for the difference in light and shadow you have here.  The background seems a bit busy but is beyond your control. The shot is just barely set up to the right a little. You may have been able to control this when cropping, but for this shot, it is pretty important. Processing seems good. My Opinion On The Photo  I originally scored this shot a seven. I think that it meets the challenge well but could have been improved a just little to finish even higher than it did. Good job, and keep up the good work.   I would be happy to talk further about this shot if you would like to contact me.DougPaz  ', '2003-03-19 05:27:50.000000', NULL, 0),
(241304, 13684, 6328, '<td valign="top">Hello Mr Setzler.<br/>Critique by Silver Fox.<br/> <br/>Certainly meets the Bridges Challenge<br/> <br/>Your photography shows great depth and detail. What I particularly like is your decision to move slightly to the left of center to take the shot. The right hand wall of the bridge as a result, serves as an excellent component on which to hang the whole composition; so many people would have taken their shot from the "centre".<br/>This also serves as your purpose in using the rule of 3rds to control your photograph.<br/> <br/>Now that''s also what i call a long Depth Of Field, showing good details a nice prespective, and excellent lighting.  <br/>Great idea for the exposure to accomplish the silhouetting of yourself and as you stated, to still leave the details visible for us to see.  Excellent idea!  <br/> <br/>I''m a lover of wooden and covered bridges (don''t get to seem them that often) and I love to look at photo''s showing the detail in the wooden planks on the floors, walls, and ceilings, and your photograph does just that to a large degree.  It shows the strength of this bridge and the wonder of the time and detail that went into building such a structure that still stands to this day.<br/> <br/>Colours are excellent, most of them are quite neutral and are coming from the wood, almost like a sepia toning of old.  The wood is weathered and old and the colours are natural, what more could you ask for.<br/> <br/>Focus is great, from front to rear.  Couldn''t be any sharper, I believe.<br/>Visual impact is pleasant to the viewers eyes.<br/> <br/>My only problem: It looks like they repaired the bridge with a piece of plywood at the bottom left of the bridge and the lighting at that end really catches it and lights it up.  Could you have cropped it out, or moved your camera forward a foot or so to cut it out without spoiling your mood?<br/> <br/>Excellent photography!</td>', 'Hello Mr Setzler.Critique by Silver Fox. Certainly meets the Bridges Challenge Your photography shows great depth and detail. What I particularly like is your decision to move slightly to the left of center to take the shot. The right hand wall of the bridge as a result, serves as an excellent component on which to hang the whole composition; so many people would have taken their shot from the "centre".This also serves as your purpose in using the rule of 3rds to control your photograph. Now that''s also what i call a long Depth Of Field, showing good details a nice prespective, and excellent lighting.  Great idea for the exposure to accomplish the silhouetting of yourself and as you stated, to still leave the details visible for us to see.  Excellent idea!   I''m a lover of wooden and covered bridges (don''t get to seem them that often) and I love to look at photo''s showing the detail in the wooden planks on the floors, walls, and ceilings, and your photograph does just that to a large degree.  It shows the strength of this bridge and the wonder of the time and detail that went into building such a structure that still stands to this day. Colours are excellent, most of them are quite neutral and are coming from the wood, almost like a sepia toning of old.  The wood is weathered and old and the colours are natural, what more could you ask for. Focus is great, from front to rear.  Couldn''t be any sharper, I believe.Visual impact is pleasant to the viewers eyes. My only problem: It looks like they repaired the bridge with a piece of plywood at the bottom left of the bridge and the lighting at that end really catches it and lights it up.  Could you have cropped it out, or moved your camera forward a foot or so to cut it out without spoiling your mood? Excellent photography!', '2003-03-19 09:56:44.000000', NULL, 0),
(241334, 13706, 6328, '<td valign="top">Greetings Josh.<br/>Critique by Silver Fox.<br/> <br/>You certainly meet the Bridges Challenge with this photograph.<br/> <br/>This is outstanding work. Great depth and detail; It''s a long DOF, good details nice prespective, excellent lighting for the tme of day, wonderful composition.  It''s one heck of a long walking bridge.<br/> <br/>Focus is just fine, nice and sharp at the front,down towards the middle as you would expect.  <br/> <br/>I like the way you''ve got the two puddles on the wood on the right side but at the same time, they could be distracting to many viewers.<br/>And I also like the way the wooden boards on both sides work as lines leading the eyes right towards the person walking at the end of the bridge.<br/> <br/>You have good exposure, and you show good tones both in the wood and steel.<br/> <br/>Excellent photography in my opinion.  Keep shooting!<br/> <br/></td>', 'Greetings Josh.Critique by Silver Fox. You certainly meet the Bridges Challenge with this photograph. This is outstanding work. Great depth and detail; It''s a long DOF, good details nice prespective, excellent lighting for the tme of day, wonderful composition.  It''s one heck of a long walking bridge. Focus is just fine, nice and sharp at the front,down towards the middle as you would expect.   I like the way you''ve got the two puddles on the wood on the right side but at the same time, they could be distracting to many viewers.And I also like the way the wooden boards on both sides work as lines leading the eyes right towards the person walking at the end of the bridge. You have good exposure, and you show good tones both in the wood and steel. Excellent photography in my opinion.  Keep shooting! ', '2003-03-19 10:44:35.000000', NULL, 0),
(241403, 13746, 6328, '<td valign="top">hello Chickadee.<br/>Critique by Silver Fox.<br/> <br/>Composition is quite good, and your photograph certainly met the Challenge.<br/>The visual impact is pleasant to the eyes leading us from the front beams up the logs and along the top of the trestle to the curve at the other end.<br/> <br/>Your focus is good and sharp leading almost to the end of the trestle.<br/> <br/>The Colour captured of the foliage both in front of the trestle and towards the back with the fog coming down from the mountain is really rich.  The colour also captures the age and discolouration of the various timbers used on this trestle.  <br/> <br/>Very well done! Would have been interesting to see a Train coming around the bend!  Keep Shooting. . .looking foward to seeing more of your work in the future.<br/> <br/>Regards,<br/>SilverFox</td>', 'hello Chickadee.Critique by Silver Fox. Composition is quite good, and your photograph certainly met the Challenge.The visual impact is pleasant to the eyes leading us from the front beams up the logs and along the top of the trestle to the curve at the other end. Your focus is good and sharp leading almost to the end of the trestle. The Colour captured of the foliage both in front of the trestle and towards the back with the fog coming down from the mountain is really rich.  The colour also captures the age and discolouration of the various timbers used on this trestle.   Very well done! Would have been interesting to see a Train coming around the bend!  Keep Shooting. . .looking foward to seeing more of your work in the future. Regards,SilverFox', '2003-03-19 12:21:27.000000', NULL, 0),
(241472, 13823, 1041, '<td valign="top"><b>A Comment From The Critique Club</b> <br/> <br/>Hi Steve!<br/> <br/>My very first impression on your photo was how the color of the bridge matches the water and just how high the bridge seems to be.  Very cool :)<br/> <br/>Your composition is good, I think under the bridge shots often work well, and it does here, too.  The metal ''parts'' make for interesting patterns without obscuring the landscape on the other side of the bridge.  Your eye is lead all the way through the picture by following the bridge, and actually finds a ''resting place'' on the other side, but doesn''t wander out of the photo, because there''s still space to the right.  Well done.<br/> <br/>The thing that lets your photo down a bit is the bland white sky.  I know you can''t exactly control the weather, and I don''t know if you could''ve come back at a different time of day (that might also eliminate the shadow on the water), but a polarizing filter helps sometimes unless the sky is just a gray mass of clouds to begin with.  If it is just a washed-out blue, the polarizer could be used to enhance the color.  Another suggestion I''ve been given on bland skies is to simply exclude the sky from the photo altogether, but I feel that wasn''t an option here for you.<br/> <br/>The photo seems just a tiny bit soft, did you use unsharpen mask?  If you didn''t or usually don''t (I know I didn''t even know what that wasuntil after I started at DPC), it is best used after you crop and resize your photo, resizing it down makes it inherently a little softer, and unsharpen mask counters that.  I believe there is a tutorial on this site, too.  Your border nicely enhances the photo without being distracting.<br/> <br/>Your entry definitely met the challenge, and has a lot of potential.  There are some things that you could improve on further, if you live close enough to the bridge you might want to go back when the weather is better for photography and take another set of shots and see what a difference it can make.<br/> <br/>Please let me know if you have any questions or comments about this review.<br/> <br/>Franziska</td>', 'A Comment From The Critique Club  Hi Steve! My very first impression on your photo was how the color of the bridge matches the water and just how high the bridge seems to be.  Very cool :) Your composition is good, I think under the bridge shots often work well, and it does here, too.  The metal ''parts'' make for interesting patterns without obscuring the landscape on the other side of the bridge.  Your eye is lead all the way through the picture by following the bridge, and actually finds a ''resting place'' on the other side, but doesn''t wander out of the photo, because there''s still space to the right.  Well done. The thing that lets your photo down a bit is the bland white sky.  I know you can''t exactly control the weather, and I don''t know if you could''ve come back at a different time of day (that might also eliminate the shadow on the water), but a polarizing filter helps sometimes unless the sky is just a gray mass of clouds to begin with.  If it is just a washed-out blue, the polarizer could be used to enhance the color.  Another suggestion I''ve been given on bland skies is to simply exclude the sky from the photo altogether, but I feel that wasn''t an option here for you. The photo seems just a tiny bit soft, did you use unsharpen mask?  If you didn''t or usually don''t (I know I didn''t even know what that wasuntil after I started at DPC), it is best used after you crop and resize your photo, resizing it down makes it inherently a little softer, and unsharpen mask counters that.  I believe there is a tutorial on this site, too.  Your border nicely enhances the photo without being distracting. Your entry definitely met the challenge, and has a lot of potential.  There are some things that you could improve on further, if you live close enough to the bridge you might want to go back when the weather is better for photography and take another set of shots and see what a difference it can make. Please let me know if you have any questions or comments about this review. Franziska', '2003-03-19 14:05:47.000000', NULL, 0),
(241513, 13776, 811, '<td valign="top">CRITIQUE CLUB CRITIQUE<br/>by karmat<br/> <br/>COMPOSITION<br/>The webbing, helps to draw the eyes from left to right to the spider and the face.  I think, however, it would have been more effective to crop the right side, so that the face was on teh edge, and the spider was on the right vertical third line.<br/> <br/>TECHNIQUE<br/>Great focus, and the sepia tones really add to this picture, I think.  Straight black and white with a lot of contrast might have been interesting as well.  <br/> <br/>OVERALL EFFECT<br/>Overall, it is a good picture.  I know when I was voting, I wondered if the spider''s web was supposed to be the bridge.  I am suspecting, from comments below, that most people wondered teh same thing.  With that thought in mind, it is a good photograph that makes people stop and think.  For some reason, I kept thinking of Halloween or a haunted house when looking at it -- it just has that mood.<br/> <br/>Best to you in future challenges.<br/> <br/>karmat</td>', 'CRITIQUE CLUB CRITIQUEby karmat COMPOSITIONThe webbing, helps to draw the eyes from left to right to the spider and the face.  I think, however, it would have been more effective to crop the right side, so that the face was on teh edge, and the spider was on the right vertical third line. TECHNIQUEGreat focus, and the sepia tones really add to this picture, I think.  Straight black and white with a lot of contrast might have been interesting as well.   OVERALL EFFECTOverall, it is a good picture.  I know when I was voting, I wondered if the spider''s web was supposed to be the bridge.  I am suspecting, from comments below, that most people wondered teh same thing.  With that thought in mind, it is a good photograph that makes people stop and think.  For some reason, I kept thinking of Halloween or a haunted house when looking at it -- it just has that mood. Best to you in future challenges. karmat', '2003-03-19 14:38:56.000000', NULL, 0),
(241526, 13903, 6527, '<td valign="top">Heya Brian,<br/> <br/>Great shot, I''m glad you made it safe and sound to your new home! Good luck out there and keep sending in these great pictures.<br/> <br/> <br/>Brad and Steph B.<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2003-03-19 15:02:43.</i></td>', 'Heya Brian, Great shot, I''m glad you made it safe and sound to your new home! Good luck out there and keep sending in these great pictures.  Brad and Steph B.', '2003-03-19 15:02:20.000000', '2003-03-19 15:02:43.000000', 0),
(241529, 13599, 527, '<td valign="top">Critique Club critique from Tim Jensen<br/> <br/>I believe you were trying to capture a semi abstract point of view that showed the detail and character of the bridge. While I can appreciate that point of view, in this case I don''t find this photo to be particularily compelling. I''m not sure why exactly but I feel that it is because it just seems ordinary. Not at all bad but it lacks the visual interest. Part of the reason is that it seems to be taken at a distance that only makes me more curious as to what the rest of bridge looks like but I feel frustration that I can''t. I do usually enjoy studying a subject very closely when there is a lot of rich texture to enjoy but I''m just not finding a lot of enjoyable textures here. Maybe if you would have done something more interesting with the composition, like taking the photo from a more a extreme angle or waited until the lighting was more dramatic that would have helped give it some punch. <br/> <br/>It is executed pretty well with sharp details and natural colors. I do like the complimentary and secondary colors used here, they are pleasing to look at. The values are interesting with the darkness underneath the bridge complimenting the brightness of the columns. <br/> <br/>You have plenty of interesting elements to work with here. I just feel that the composition is the weakest area that needs improvement. <br/> <br/>Tim</td>', 'Critique Club critique from Tim Jensen I believe you were trying to capture a semi abstract point of view that showed the detail and character of the bridge. While I can appreciate that point of view, in this case I don''t find this photo to be particularily compelling. I''m not sure why exactly but I feel that it is because it just seems ordinary. Not at all bad but it lacks the visual interest. Part of the reason is that it seems to be taken at a distance that only makes me more curious as to what the rest of bridge looks like but I feel frustration that I can''t. I do usually enjoy studying a subject very closely when there is a lot of rich texture to enjoy but I''m just not finding a lot of enjoyable textures here. Maybe if you would have done something more interesting with the composition, like taking the photo from a more a extreme angle or waited until the lighting was more dramatic that would have helped give it some punch.  It is executed pretty well with sharp details and natural colors. I do like the complimentary and secondary colors used here, they are pleasing to look at. The values are interesting with the darkness underneath the bridge complimenting the brightness of the columns.  You have plenty of interesting elements to work with here. I just feel that the composition is the weakest area that needs improvement.  Tim', '2003-03-19 15:07:16.000000', NULL, 0),
(241530, 13697, 811, '<td valign="top">CRITIQUE CLUB CRITIQUE<br/>by karmat<br/> <br/>COMPOSITION<br/>I like how you have composed this shot so that the bridges lead the eyes through the frame, and then rest on teh structure on the right.  It is a neat contrast of the bridges.<br/> <br/>TECHNIQUE<br/>Your focus does seem to be a bit off.  Were you using a tripod?  If not, and you don''t have one, a lot of times you can get by with using a bag with flour, rice, or something like that in it as a portable bean bag.  Put the bag on something, then rest the camera on it.  this eliminates any camera/hand shake.  Or, since it looks like it was a bright day, I would also try to use a fast shutter speed.<br/> <br/>You have some nice colors, but overall teh picture is a little flat.  Maybe add some saturation or play with the contrast levels some if you can.  That may help bring out the details in your picture.  Also, if you have a polarizer, that may have helped your colors have more depth to them.<br/> <br/>OVERALL EFFECT<br/>This is a nice picture to look at, and you have met the challenge well.  <br/> <br/>Best to you in future challenges.<br/>karmat</td>', 'CRITIQUE CLUB CRITIQUEby karmat COMPOSITIONI like how you have composed this shot so that the bridges lead the eyes through the frame, and then rest on teh structure on the right.  It is a neat contrast of the bridges. TECHNIQUEYour focus does seem to be a bit off.  Were you using a tripod?  If not, and you don''t have one, a lot of times you can get by with using a bag with flour, rice, or something like that in it as a portable bean bag.  Put the bag on something, then rest the camera on it.  this eliminates any camera/hand shake.  Or, since it looks like it was a bright day, I would also try to use a fast shutter speed. You have some nice colors, but overall teh picture is a little flat.  Maybe add some saturation or play with the contrast levels some if you can.  That may help bring out the details in your picture.  Also, if you have a polarizer, that may have helped your colors have more depth to them. OVERALL EFFECTThis is a nice picture to look at, and you have met the challenge well.   Best to you in future challenges.karmat', '2003-03-19 15:10:52.000000', NULL, 0),
(241537, 13544, 811, '<td valign="top">CRITIQUE CLUB CRITIQUE<br/>by karmat<br/> <br/>COMPOSITION<br/>I really like how the picture "starts" on the lower left, and takes the eyes up the stairs, back to the left, and then over the bridge.  It definitely allows the eyes some movement in the picture, without being overly busy or cluttered.  Probably because of the way it is colored, though, the sign becomes the main focal point to me, instead of the bridge.  It just kinda jumps out at me.<br/>Would it have been possible to crop the pole out some way?  It kinda distracts from the overall quality, I think.<br/>Someone below mentioned moving some to the left.  If you could have gotten more of the expanse, in addition to the steps, I think that would have been really effective.<br/> <br/>TECHNIQUE<br/>The focus and lighting work well together to give this picture an "aged" look, which suits the subject well.  <br/> <br/>OVERALL EFFECT<br/>Your composition, and use of sepia, really gives the impression of "CLOSED" here to me, os I think you have done a good job.  Some minor things in teh general composition may have made it a stronger picture for more of the viewers, however.<br/> <br/>Best to you in future challenges.<br/>karmat</td>', 'CRITIQUE CLUB CRITIQUEby karmat COMPOSITIONI really like how the picture "starts" on the lower left, and takes the eyes up the stairs, back to the left, and then over the bridge.  It definitely allows the eyes some movement in the picture, without being overly busy or cluttered.  Probably because of the way it is colored, though, the sign becomes the main focal point to me, instead of the bridge.  It just kinda jumps out at me.Would it have been possible to crop the pole out some way?  It kinda distracts from the overall quality, I think.Someone below mentioned moving some to the left.  If you could have gotten more of the expanse, in addition to the steps, I think that would have been really effective. TECHNIQUEThe focus and lighting work well together to give this picture an "aged" look, which suits the subject well.   OVERALL EFFECTYour composition, and use of sepia, really gives the impression of "CLOSED" here to me, os I think you have done a good job.  Some minor things in teh general composition may have made it a stronger picture for more of the viewers, however. Best to you in future challenges.karmat', '2003-03-19 15:29:48.000000', NULL, 0),
(241548, 13873, 472, '<td valign="top">excellent .. fog rules! :)</td>', 'excellent .. fog rules! :)', '2003-03-19 15:47:09.000000', NULL, 0),
(241568, 13611, 811, '<td valign="top">CRITIQUE CLUB CRITIQUE<br/>by karmat<br/> <br/>COMPOSITION<br/>The composition of this works well.  It takes your eyes across the frame in a simple, but effective manner.  I think something else to explore, if you could get close enough, is to compose it or frame it in such a way as to highlight the crumbling part of the bridge, as that is very interesting.<br/> <br/>TECHNIQUE<br/>Your focus is right on, as I can see the details of the bridge and surrounding terrain.  It is a bit overexposed in the upper right hand corner.  Would it have been possible to photograph at another angle or time of the day?<br/> <br/>OVERALL EFFECT<br/>The sepia tone in this works very well, I think, to add to the "decaying" feel of the picture.  Nicely done, and congrats on your first entry.  Hope you enjoy DPC!<br/> <br/>Best to you in future challenges.<br/> <br/>karmat</td>', 'CRITIQUE CLUB CRITIQUEby karmat COMPOSITIONThe composition of this works well.  It takes your eyes across the frame in a simple, but effective manner.  I think something else to explore, if you could get close enough, is to compose it or frame it in such a way as to highlight the crumbling part of the bridge, as that is very interesting. TECHNIQUEYour focus is right on, as I can see the details of the bridge and surrounding terrain.  It is a bit overexposed in the upper right hand corner.  Would it have been possible to photograph at another angle or time of the day? OVERALL EFFECTThe sepia tone in this works very well, I think, to add to the "decaying" feel of the picture.  Nicely done, and congrats on your first entry.  Hope you enjoy DPC! Best to you in future challenges. karmat', '2003-03-19 16:01:53.000000', NULL, 0),
(241635, 13531, 1041, '<td valign="top"><b>A Comment From The Critique Club</b> <br/> <br/>Hi Mark!<br/> <br/>My immediate reaction was ''Home''!  I lived in London for six years so it was exciting for me to see a photo of Tower Bridge :)<br/> <br/>You''ve chosen a great subject, and you are doing it justice.  Tower Bridge is very impressive and the angle that you have chosen is nice.  I wonder what your original looked like, if it allows it I would suggest a slightly different crop:  Leave a little more room at the top so that the tip of the tower at the front is visible.  Crop the right side a bit more to remove the beginnings of the entrance sign.  This would achieve a couple of things in my mind: balance the shape of your photo a bit more (at the moment it is squarish but not quite square, and that just feels a bit off when I look at it).  It would also remove more white sky (which doesn''t add anything to your photo) and the half-legible word (the viewer''s eyes are always drawn to writing which in your photo is not the main subject).   I do like the angle that you''ve chosen (not having the tower straight), and I noticed that the voters are divided on it.  I think you did well choosing this angle because that avoids the converging lines issue by making non-vertical lines a feature rather than a problem.  :)<br/> <br/>Was it the sky that made you choose to go b&amp;w (or slightly toned, I think, at least on my monitor here)?  Whatever it was, I think the choice is an excellent one.  Color photos of Tower Bridge are much more common and your choice of b&amp;w sets your photo apart from the rest. <br/> <br/>The tonal range and detail you have achieved in your photo is exemplary, you have everything from pure black to pure white and all the details in the bridge itself are nicely visible, and there are no blown-out or underexposed areas.  The white sky is nice and simple and works well, but I still think it wouldn''t hurt to see less of it (see my earlier comment above).  Everything''s nice and sharp, too.  Good work.<br/> <br/>The photo definitely meets the challenge, the bridge is the obvious (and only) focus of your entry.  I like it very much, and overall, the voters did, too.  This was a tough challenge with lots of good competition and I think you held up really well with your entry.  <br/> <br/>I just checked out your portfolio, b&amp;w is obviously a favorite of yours and you do it well.  I''m looking forward to seeing more of that from you :)<br/> <br/>Please let me know if you have any questions or comments about this review.<br/> <br/>Franziska.</td>', 'A Comment From The Critique Club  Hi Mark! My immediate reaction was ''Home''!  I lived in London for six years so it was exciting for me to see a photo of Tower Bridge :) You''ve chosen a great subject, and you are doing it justice.  Tower Bridge is very impressive and the angle that you have chosen is nice.  I wonder what your original looked like, if it allows it I would suggest a slightly different crop:  Leave a little more room at the top so that the tip of the tower at the front is visible.  Crop the right side a bit more to remove the beginnings of the entrance sign.  This would achieve a couple of things in my mind: balance the shape of your photo a bit more (at the moment it is squarish but not quite square, and that just feels a bit off when I look at it).  It would also remove more white sky (which doesn''t add anything to your photo) and the half-legible word (the viewer''s eyes are always drawn to writing which in your photo is not the main subject).   I do like the angle that you''ve chosen (not having the tower straight), and I noticed that the voters are divided on it.  I think you did well choosing this angle because that avoids the converging lines issue by making non-vertical lines a feature rather than a problem.  :) Was it the sky that made you choose to go b&w (or slightly toned, I think, at least on my monitor here)?  Whatever it was, I think the choice is an excellent one.  Color photos of Tower Bridge are much more common and your choice of b&w sets your photo apart from the rest.  The tonal range and detail you have achieved in your photo is exemplary, you have everything from pure black to pure white and all the details in the bridge itself are nicely visible, and there are no blown-out or underexposed areas.  The white sky is nice and simple and works well, but I still think it wouldn''t hurt to see less of it (see my earlier comment above).  Everything''s nice and sharp, too.  Good work. The photo definitely meets the challenge, the bridge is the obvious (and only) focus of your entry.  I like it very much, and overall, the voters did, too.  This was a tough challenge with lots of good competition and I think you held up really well with your entry.   I just checked out your portfolio, b&w is obviously a favorite of yours and you do it well.  I''m looking forward to seeing more of that from you :) Please let me know if you have any questions or comments about this review. Franziska.', '2003-03-19 16:55:02.000000', NULL, 0),
(241637, 13663, 327, '<td valign="top"><b>CC</b> <br/>Hello and welcome Robster......your first challenge!<br/> <br/>Fits The Challenge- Sure if the photographer thinks so and calls this Katherine''s bridge then that is what it is. Time will tell as we see more photos from this artist. <br/>Composition- I like it.<br/>Background- I like the natural setting for this nature shot. <br/>My Opinion- I like the soft focus/noise. Giving the benefit of doubt, it''s intended here. You know nudes don''t have to be so _in_ your face 100% of the time. This all works, the colors, the girl....all soft and muted. Nice job.<br/> <br/></td>', 'CC Hello and welcome Robster......your first challenge! Fits The Challenge- Sure if the photographer thinks so and calls this Katherine''s bridge then that is what it is. Time will tell as we see more photos from this artist. Composition- I like it.Background- I like the natural setting for this nature shot. My Opinion- I like the soft focus/noise. Giving the benefit of doubt, it''s intended here. You know nudes don''t have to be so _in_ your face 100% of the time. This all works, the colors, the girl....all soft and muted. Nice job. ', '2003-03-19 16:56:44.000000', NULL, 0),
(241645, 13603, 6328, '<td valign="top">The Royal Gorge, by vtruan<br/>Greetings:  Critique by Silver Fox <br/> <br/> <br/>Interesting composition; gorgeous scenery taken during the daylight hours with the bright sun shining off the Gorge walls doesn\''t allow you much room to manipulate your settings with all that brightness.<br/> <br/>I like the way you\''ve tried to frame the photograph with the tree below you and the branch above you, a very good idea, using nature this way.<br/> <br/>Very interesting having all that snow on the other side of the Gorge and the Sun shining on the opposite side.  Overall, the contrast and depth of field are great, and your focusing is right on. . .<br/> <br/>This is a nice looking bridge with a great looking landscape, but I think you\''ve taken a larger shot than needed, getting the bridge lost with it\''s surroundings.  I wonder perhaps if you should have zoomed in and presented the bridge a little more on it\''s own, and perhaps cut out the trees.  It\''s really hard to say, because I wasn\''t there taking this photograph, you were.<br/> <br/>One thing that I\''ve learned in taking photographs of people and landscapes is to keep it simple, no distractions in the background or foreground and keep your camera focused on that which caught your eye to start with.  Good luck on your next Challenge.<br/>Regards,<br/>Ed<br/></td>', 'The Royal Gorge, by vtruanGreetings:  Critique by Silver Fox   Interesting composition; gorgeous scenery taken during the daylight hours with the bright sun shining off the Gorge walls doesn\''t allow you much room to manipulate your settings with all that brightness. I like the way you\''ve tried to frame the photograph with the tree below you and the branch above you, a very good idea, using nature this way. Very interesting having all that snow on the other side of the Gorge and the Sun shining on the opposite side.  Overall, the contrast and depth of field are great, and your focusing is right on. . . This is a nice looking bridge with a great looking landscape, but I think you\''ve taken a larger shot than needed, getting the bridge lost with it\''s surroundings.  I wonder perhaps if you should have zoomed in and presented the bridge a little more on it\''s own, and perhaps cut out the trees.  It\''s really hard to say, because I wasn\''t there taking this photograph, you were. One thing that I\''ve learned in taking photographs of people and landscapes is to keep it simple, no distractions in the background or foreground and keep your camera focused on that which caught your eye to start with.  Good luck on your next Challenge.Regards,Ed', '2003-03-19 17:27:04.000000', NULL, 0),
(241848, 13687, 6328, '<td valign="top">Greetings Frank.<br/>Critique by Silver Fox.<br/> <br/>Youve certainly taken a photograph of a lovely old landscape that Im sure you thought would meet the Challenge, but with no bridge in sight, Im not so sure.<br/> <br/>The colors of the brickwork come out just lovely, and I can just imagine the labor and work that would have gone into making such a building in the days that this dates back to.  Is there a moat all around the Castle?<br/> <br/>Your focusing is fine for what you show us, but what you do show is very limited to the corner of the building and the railings, plus the trees across the way.  <br/> <br/>You would have been much better to shoot across by the trees and show us the entire Castle with the bridge so we would have something more with which to compare and judge by.<br/> <br/>I read in one of the comments below about your photo being too dark, but with the time of year and the weather permitting in various parts of the world, we are limited with what we get by Mother Nature.  Of course, once at home we can always lighten it up on our computer, but even I am still trying to learn these things.<br/> <br/>Your composition is good and strong and your focus also works for you as I said above.  Your thought is creative and unique and your dof is quite good.  But there is nothing really in your photo to grab my attention.<br/> <br/>This is only your first entry into the DP Challenge, so take your time, look at other photographs that have been entered into other Challenges, go out and shoot some more, enter your shots into more Challenges, learn more, keep shooting, and I hope to see more of your work next month and the month after that too.<br/>Regards,<br/>Ed<br/></td>', 'Greetings Frank.Critique by Silver Fox. Youve certainly taken a photograph of a lovely old landscape that Im sure you thought would meet the Challenge, but with no bridge in sight, Im not so sure. The colors of the brickwork come out just lovely, and I can just imagine the labor and work that would have gone into making such a building in the days that this dates back to.  Is there a moat all around the Castle? Your focusing is fine for what you show us, but what you do show is very limited to the corner of the building and the railings, plus the trees across the way.   You would have been much better to shoot across by the trees and show us the entire Castle with the bridge so we would have something more with which to compare and judge by. I read in one of the comments below about your photo being too dark, but with the time of year and the weather permitting in various parts of the world, we are limited with what we get by Mother Nature.  Of course, once at home we can always lighten it up on our computer, but even I am still trying to learn these things. Your composition is good and strong and your focus also works for you as I said above.  Your thought is creative and unique and your dof is quite good.  But there is nothing really in your photo to grab my attention. This is only your first entry into the DP Challenge, so take your time, look at other photographs that have been entered into other Challenges, go out and shoot some more, enter your shots into more Challenges, learn more, keep shooting, and I hope to see more of your work next month and the month after that too.Regards,Ed', '2003-03-19 21:50:26.000000', NULL, 0),
(241871, 13835, 6900, '<td valign="top">Hurts my head - sorry for the low score, but I can''t LOOK at it.</td>', 'Hurts my head - sorry for the low score, but I can''t LOOK at it.', '2003-03-19 22:17:11.000000', NULL, 0),
(242004, 13551, 6458, '<td valign="top">Thanks Kim!<br/>I tend not to rate my own photos very highly and I''m aware of the "fickle" nature of the DPC voters. I figured the barbed wire and the lack of "Bridge" would put some people off (as evidenced the 7 people that voted this pic as 3 and under).<br/> <br/>Those that are interested can check out the outtakes from this challenge in <a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/portfolio.php?USER_ID=6458&amp;collection_id=260" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">this</a> portfolio, these pictures show more of the bridge and you can get a sense of how it fits. "Escher Re-visted" was almost entered instead of "Infinite Support" I was torn between the two.<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2003-03-20 07:02:37.</i></td>', 'Thanks Kim!I tend not to rate my own photos very highly and I''m aware of the "fickle" nature of the DPC voters. I figured the barbed wire and the lack of "Bridge" would put some people off (as evidenced the 7 people that voted this pic as 3 and under). Those that are interested can check out the outtakes from this challenge in this portfolio, these pictures show more of the bridge and you can get a sense of how it fits. "Escher Re-visted" was almost entered instead of "Infinite Support" I was torn between the two.', '2003-03-20 07:00:29.000000', '2003-03-20 07:02:37.000000', 0),
(242041, 13594, 772, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club :)<br/> <br/>Hi Rafter...<br/> <br/>I love the snow that you get in Canada... I live in an area that gets ''some'' snow but not usually large amounts at any given time.  Since I love snow, i really enjoy seeing nice photos of it :)  <br/> <br/>I really like the way you have chosen to compose this photo.  The bridge seems to be nicely located in the lower portion of the frame.  I really like your creative choice to ''frame'' the bridge with the snow covered branches in the foreground of this image.  This pushes my eye directly to the bridge :)  <br/> <br/>The subdued color of this snow scene also does a nice job of enhancing the feel of a cold winter afternoon.  I can envision red noses and bundled up children standing behind you waiting for you to finish this photo so they can sled on the hill :)  <br/> <br/>Keep up the great work :)<br/> <br/>John Setzler<br/></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club :) Hi Rafter... I love the snow that you get in Canada... I live in an area that gets ''some'' snow but not usually large amounts at any given time.  Since I love snow, i really enjoy seeing nice photos of it :)   I really like the way you have chosen to compose this photo.  The bridge seems to be nicely located in the lower portion of the frame.  I really like your creative choice to ''frame'' the bridge with the snow covered branches in the foreground of this image.  This pushes my eye directly to the bridge :)   The subdued color of this snow scene also does a nice job of enhancing the feel of a cold winter afternoon.  I can envision red noses and bundled up children standing behind you waiting for you to finish this photo so they can sled on the hill :)   Keep up the great work :) John Setzler', '2003-03-20 08:45:56.000000', NULL, 0),
(242078, 13727, 6328, '<td valign="top">Ducks Under Bridge<br/>Hi Rachel,<br/>Critique by Silver Fox.<br/> <br/>Your composition is quite good here with good depth of field and a good idea for the Challenge.<br/> <br/>The Visual impact is pleasant to the eyes of the beholder when looking at your picture.  The rushing water heading towards the bridge gives <br/>the ducks motion as they keep themselves heading upstream..<br/> <br/>Focus is quite good, and the lighting seems fine for a daylight shot. <br/> <br/>Your choice of Color seems okay, sepia tone appears just right, wonder what this would have looked like in colour.<br/> <br/>The addition of the ivy hanging from the bridge gives the photograph something more to look at, however, because the theme of Challenge was Bridges you may have been better to back up about 10 feet and given us a better view of the bridge instead of concentrating so much on the three ducks.  They would still have been in the photograph, but not have been the main subject.  <br/> <br/>The bridge looks like it has some beauty to the rails and it would have been nice to see more of them.<br/> <br/>Now when you cropped your photograph, what happened to the bottom right hand side, what is that white washed out piece?  Could you not have taken that out?  Or is that why you had to come so close with your camera and lens to start with?<br/> <br/>Overall, you''ve taken a nice photograph to be proud of and you''ve done a nice job of it too.With a little practice, you''ll be pulling out 10''s in no time.<br/> <br/>I noticed that youve just joined the DP Challenge, so dont take this critique personally.  <br/> <br/>Its only your first entry.  Take some time and look at the other photos in the Galleries, and then shoot some more and some more and I would enjoy seeing some more of your work next week and the week after that too.  <br/>And remember to have some fun while you learn. . .<br/>Sincerely,<br/>Ed<br/></td>', 'Ducks Under BridgeHi Rachel,Critique by Silver Fox. Your composition is quite good here with good depth of field and a good idea for the Challenge. The Visual impact is pleasant to the eyes of the beholder when looking at your picture.  The rushing water heading towards the bridge gives the ducks motion as they keep themselves heading upstream.. Focus is quite good, and the lighting seems fine for a daylight shot.  Your choice of Color seems okay, sepia tone appears just right, wonder what this would have looked like in colour. The addition of the ivy hanging from the bridge gives the photograph something more to look at, however, because the theme of Challenge was Bridges you may have been better to back up about 10 feet and given us a better view of the bridge instead of concentrating so much on the three ducks.  They would still have been in the photograph, but not have been the main subject.   The bridge looks like it has some beauty to the rails and it would have been nice to see more of them. Now when you cropped your photograph, what happened to the bottom right hand side, what is that white washed out piece?  Could you not have taken that out?  Or is that why you had to come so close with your camera and lens to start with? Overall, you''ve taken a nice photograph to be proud of and you''ve done a nice job of it too.With a little practice, you''ll be pulling out 10''s in no time. I noticed that youve just joined the DP Challenge, so dont take this critique personally.   Its only your first entry.  Take some time and look at the other photos in the Galleries, and then shoot some more and some more and I would enjoy seeing some more of your work next week and the week after that too.  And remember to have some fun while you learn. . .Sincerely,Ed', '2003-03-20 11:11:32.000000', NULL, 0),
(242108, 13876, 6328, '<td valign="top">3D by Paige<br/> <br/>Hi Paige,<br/>Critique by Silver Fox<br/> <br/>First, let me congratulate you on this photograph.  It scored pretty high on the Challenge and proved to be a favorite.<br/> <br/>The composition is excellent and has great depth of field and contrast.  <br/> <br/>The colors are excellent with the blue sky and hills in the background and then the buildings of the City and the blue waters of the bay.  <br/> <br/>In my opinion being able to get the color of the steel cables into your photograph were an extra for the viewer to see.  <br/> <br/>Unfortunately for me, I did not see this the first time around, and I only gave you a four.<br/> <br/>I also think the addition of the Steel Column at the right is a magnificent show of power and strength.<br/> <br/>Focus: theres nothing to say, everything is fine, the cables look softly in focus, the steel column is great and everything else is just dandy, a great job.<br/> <br/>For lighting  what can one say, its a daytime shot, the sun is out, theres smog around, you do the best with what you have, and thats <br/>exactly what youve done here.<br/> <br/>Ive checked your home page out and you have some great photographs there, so keep up the good work and keep having fun.  Hope this piece of work finds a place on a wall in your home.<br/> <br/>Regards,<br/>Ed<br/> <br/>Ps. Please start giving us your aperture/shutter info.as it would help a lot in our critique''s. Thanks<br/></td>', '3D by Paige Hi Paige,Critique by Silver Fox First, let me congratulate you on this photograph.  It scored pretty high on the Challenge and proved to be a favorite. The composition is excellent and has great depth of field and contrast.   The colors are excellent with the blue sky and hills in the background and then the buildings of the City and the blue waters of the bay.   In my opinion being able to get the color of the steel cables into your photograph were an extra for the viewer to see.   Unfortunately for me, I did not see this the first time around, and I only gave you a four. I also think the addition of the Steel Column at the right is a magnificent show of power and strength. Focus: theres nothing to say, everything is fine, the cables look softly in focus, the steel column is great and everything else is just dandy, a great job. For lighting  what can one say, its a daytime shot, the sun is out, theres smog around, you do the best with what you have, and thats exactly what youve done here. Ive checked your home page out and you have some great photographs there, so keep up the good work and keep having fun.  Hope this piece of work finds a place on a wall in your home. Regards,Ed Ps. Please start giving us your aperture/shutter info.as it would help a lot in our critique''s. Thanks', '2003-03-20 11:52:38.000000', NULL, 0),
(242304, 13718, 1143, '<td valign="top">I dont think that the subject stands out enough because it is the same color as all of the trees. It is also too centered in the frame. Please respond to my email if you would like a more detailed comment.</td>', 'I dont think that the subject stands out enough because it is the same color as all of the trees. It is also too centered in the frame. Please respond to my email if you would like a more detailed comment.', '2003-03-20 16:22:18.000000', NULL, 0),
(242307, 13580, 1143, '<td valign="top">I think that the score here reflects a few things. First, this photo isn''t really OF a bridge. The title says that it is, but mainly I see a fence and some trees though I could probably guess that it was taken from on top of a bridge. Secondly, it just isn''t too interesting. I can appreciate that the effect added some interest, but probably hurt it even more since many people do not like that sort of post processing manipulation. The diagonals created by the fence and the road below are nice lines, but there''s little else going on. Overall, nice effort.</td>', 'I think that the score here reflects a few things. First, this photo isn''t really OF a bridge. The title says that it is, but mainly I see a fence and some trees though I could probably guess that it was taken from on top of a bridge. Secondly, it just isn''t too interesting. I can appreciate that the effect added some interest, but probably hurt it even more since many people do not like that sort of post processing manipulation. The diagonals created by the fence and the road below are nice lines, but there''s little else going on. Overall, nice effort.', '2003-03-20 16:29:50.000000', NULL, 0),
(242400, 13873, 5792, '<td valign="top">Congratulations on your #1.<br/> <br/>Yours is the first image I''ve ever made one of my top three choices for the week the instant I saw it. I bet lots of people did.<br/> <br/>Everything about this image is great. You are to be commended.</td>', 'Congratulations on your #1. Yours is the first image I''ve ever made one of my top three choices for the week the instant I saw it. I bet lots of people did. Everything about this image is great. You are to be commended.', '2003-03-20 19:14:33.000000', NULL, 0),
(242626, 13873, 675, '<td valign="top">i love the focus on this photo, that must have been pretty tought to pull off with all the fog. nice job</td>', 'i love the focus on this photo, that must have been pretty tought to pull off with all the fog. nice job', '2003-03-21 10:57:21.000000', NULL, 0),
(242895, 13729, 6328, '<td valign="top">Just a walk in the Park<br/>Hi Musicman.<br/>Critique by Silver Fox.<br/> <br/> <br/>Composition is fine and a good photograph for the Bridges Challenge.<br/> <br/>Visual interest is pretty good, and you have good focus.  I especially like the reflections in the water of the bridge and trees. <br/> <br/>Your photograph shows a nice wide angle of the bridge and scenery but you should have backed up a bit and included the other side of the bridge/or cropped a little less on the right hand side to include the right abutment.<br/> <br/>The colour of your photograph is good for this time of year as there is not much color available on the trees or bushes yet, unless you want to do something with it on the computer.  This would be a beautiful photograph to take in the fall.<br/> <br/>Not a bad score or placement for your first Challenge Entry.  <br/> <br/>Keep up the good work, get out and keep shooting, look forward to seeing more of your shots in the coming weeks.<br/> <br/>Regards,<br/>Ed<br/></td>', 'Just a walk in the ParkHi Musicman.Critique by Silver Fox.  Composition is fine and a good photograph for the Bridges Challenge. Visual interest is pretty good, and you have good focus.  I especially like the reflections in the water of the bridge and trees.  Your photograph shows a nice wide angle of the bridge and scenery but you should have backed up a bit and included the other side of the bridge/or cropped a little less on the right hand side to include the right abutment. The colour of your photograph is good for this time of year as there is not much color available on the trees or bushes yet, unless you want to do something with it on the computer.  This would be a beautiful photograph to take in the fall. Not a bad score or placement for your first Challenge Entry.   Keep up the good work, get out and keep shooting, look forward to seeing more of your shots in the coming weeks. Regards,Ed', '2003-03-21 19:07:29.000000', NULL, 0),
(242896, 13678, 1280, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the CC!<br/>Gerard, what a wonderful subject you chose.  I love the juxaposition of old and new.  You composed this image well to have the new frame the old.<br/>The lighting is good here, so apparantly you chose the right time of day too, and the reflections work very well, on the new bridge, and the shadows in the water.  <br/>Your exposure is right on target.  Details are visible, and there are no burned out areas or too dark.<br/>Everything seems very well balanced, and symetric.  <br/>That old stone bridge looks very interesting and I bet it has lots of character too.  One nitpicky thing is that the old bridge is cut off the top a bit way over in the left hand side. Tilting the camera ever so slightly may have corrected that, but it''s no big thing.<br/>This is a fine image with no digital noise, good focus, exposure, composition.<br/>Good work!<br/>Regards,<br/> <br/>Grayce<br/> <br/>Do you live in Belfast?  My husband''s neice married a wonderful fellow from Belfast.  He lives in the US now, but a lot of his family is back in Ireland.</td>', 'Greetings from the CC!Gerard, what a wonderful subject you chose.  I love the juxaposition of old and new.  You composed this image well to have the new frame the old.The lighting is good here, so apparantly you chose the right time of day too, and the reflections work very well, on the new bridge, and the shadows in the water.  Your exposure is right on target.  Details are visible, and there are no burned out areas or too dark.Everything seems very well balanced, and symetric.  That old stone bridge looks very interesting and I bet it has lots of character too.  One nitpicky thing is that the old bridge is cut off the top a bit way over in the left hand side. Tilting the camera ever so slightly may have corrected that, but it''s no big thing.This is a fine image with no digital noise, good focus, exposure, composition.Good work!Regards, Grayce Do you live in Belfast?  My husband''s neice married a wonderful fellow from Belfast.  He lives in the US now, but a lot of his family is back in Ireland.', '2003-03-21 19:07:51.000000', NULL, 0),
(242899, 13644, 1280, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club!<br/>This is filled with charm.  It''s a bit like peeking into the past when viewing one of these relics.<br/>I see, by reading the comments, that some people are bothered by the foreground being in the way.   I don''t quite feel the same way.  Because there is some over-growth of vegetation the picture is more realistic.  Though they may be distracting a bit, they contribute to the theme of the bridge no longer being in service.<br/>Exposure and focus are both good, with dof very good, and no over or under exposed spots. The tone, and colours are fine as well.<br/>I''d like to have a little more water in the picture.  There is a touch over on the left, but I think a tad more in view would give a better sense of place.<br/>All in all I think you did a good job and I like this.<br/>Regards,<br/> <br/>Grayce</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club!This is filled with charm.  It''s a bit like peeking into the past when viewing one of these relics.I see, by reading the comments, that some people are bothered by the foreground being in the way.   I don''t quite feel the same way.  Because there is some over-growth of vegetation the picture is more realistic.  Though they may be distracting a bit, they contribute to the theme of the bridge no longer being in service.Exposure and focus are both good, with dof very good, and no over or under exposed spots. The tone, and colours are fine as well.I''d like to have a little more water in the picture.  There is a touch over on the left, but I think a tad more in view would give a better sense of place.All in all I think you did a good job and I like this.Regards, Grayce', '2003-03-21 19:18:38.000000', NULL, 0),
(242939, 13619, 4811, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club!...<br/> <br/>COMPOSITION... The scene is well exposed and lit, nice vivid colors...nice deep blue sky,,, if you''re not using a polarizer you should try it and see how bluer the sky turns out :) Two things I''d mention composition-wise... The airplane is way too small  to make much of an impact in the scene. I would have waited for it to pass. If it were conveniently flying overhead just as you snapped the shutter, that would be another story :) I also find that the orange overpass in the background detracts and clutters up the scene. My crop would be a little lower at the top, and cropped on  the right just enough to exclude the orange overpass. I think that even results in a nice leading line in the image...your main bridge would then extend from the left border in the foreground to the right border in the background, strengthening the composition. I like the foreground objects,,,they add interest and almost frame the bridge. Too bad there wasn''t overhanging branches of a tree or something similar to complete the frame :)<br/> <br/>TECHNIQUE... Light''s a little harsh and direct. Some evening or morning light would be excellent. Dramatic clouds too but you can''t control the weather :)  Nice sharpness, excellent image quality.<br/> <br/>OVERALL... With a little cropping I think the composition can even be better, but still a nice colorful shot.  I always try to keep elements that are too small to see out of the composition, unless they are part of a larger scheme, like people in a crowd...</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club!... COMPOSITION... The scene is well exposed and lit, nice vivid colors...nice deep blue sky,,, if you''re not using a polarizer you should try it and see how bluer the sky turns out :) Two things I''d mention composition-wise... The airplane is way too small  to make much of an impact in the scene. I would have waited for it to pass. If it were conveniently flying overhead just as you snapped the shutter, that would be another story :) I also find that the orange overpass in the background detracts and clutters up the scene. My crop would be a little lower at the top, and cropped on  the right just enough to exclude the orange overpass. I think that even results in a nice leading line in the image...your main bridge would then extend from the left border in the foreground to the right border in the background, strengthening the composition. I like the foreground objects,,,they add interest and almost frame the bridge. Too bad there wasn''t overhanging branches of a tree or something similar to complete the frame :) TECHNIQUE... Light''s a little harsh and direct. Some evening or morning light would be excellent. Dramatic clouds too but you can''t control the weather :)  Nice sharpness, excellent image quality. OVERALL... With a little cropping I think the composition can even be better, but still a nice colorful shot.  I always try to keep elements that are too small to see out of the composition, unless they are part of a larger scheme, like people in a crowd...', '2003-03-21 21:48:23.000000', NULL, 0),
(242941, 13564, 6328, '<td valign="top">Brooklyn Bridge<br/>By dimitrii<br/>Critique by Silver Fox<br/> <br/>Composition was interesting and the use of the fisheye lens was an excellent idea for this photograph.<br/> <br/>The visual appeal of the black and white (would also like to see this in color) image of the Archway, the Skyline, and the Clouds/Sky, is fantastic.<br/> <br/>Focus is nice and sharp in the whole photograph, but especially on the Arch and Wires.<br/> <br/>Photograph shows good imagination and creativeness.<br/> <br/>The angle and lens that you used here to create this photograph was interesting and adds a dimension that Ive never seen before in digital photography.  <br/> <br/>Finishing 13 out of 229 is not an easy thing to accomplish - Congratulations!  <br/>I hope to see more of your work in the weeks to come.<br/> <br/>Regards,<br/>Ed<br/></td>', 'Brooklyn BridgeBy dimitriiCritique by Silver Fox Composition was interesting and the use of the fisheye lens was an excellent idea for this photograph. The visual appeal of the black and white (would also like to see this in color) image of the Archway, the Skyline, and the Clouds/Sky, is fantastic. Focus is nice and sharp in the whole photograph, but especially on the Arch and Wires. Photograph shows good imagination and creativeness. The angle and lens that you used here to create this photograph was interesting and adds a dimension that Ive never seen before in digital photography.   Finishing 13 out of 229 is not an easy thing to accomplish - Congratulations!  I hope to see more of your work in the weeks to come. Regards,Ed', '2003-03-21 22:05:55.000000', NULL, 0),
(242955, 13680, 6328, '<td valign="top">The Bridge at the end of my Strat<br/>Roseytone<br/>Critique by Silver Fox<br/> <br/>Composition in my opinion is splendid.  <br/> <br/>The photograph in this case certainly meets the Challenge and shows some originality and newness of thought on your behalf.<br/> <br/>Im going to get a bit negative right away. I dont like your shadow in the picture.(is that you?) It distracts, and you shouldnt be there!  Now the trees -  thats a bit different, their a bit more acceptable, but youre from the U.K., arent there a lot of bridges over there that you could have included as a reflection on the guitar??  And what are the two poles (?) beside you?<br/> <br/>Visual impact has a great appeal, especially with the colors, beautiful bright Red, nice clean White, good focus and sharpness on the strings and the screws and the rest of the hardware.<br/>The black background is also a nice choice.<br/> <br/>Im really sorry to see you score so low in this Challenge.  All I can say is to keep it simple the next time and get rid of the distracting objects, which catch the voters eyes.  Hope this helps!<br/>Hope to see more of your photographs next week, have fun at it,<br/>Regards,<br/>Ed<br/></td>', 'The Bridge at the end of my StratRoseytoneCritique by Silver Fox Composition in my opinion is splendid.   The photograph in this case certainly meets the Challenge and shows some originality and newness of thought on your behalf. Im going to get a bit negative right away. I dont like your shadow in the picture.(is that you?) It distracts, and you shouldnt be there!  Now the trees -  thats a bit different, their a bit more acceptable, but youre from the U.K., arent there a lot of bridges over there that you could have included as a reflection on the guitar??  And what are the two poles (?) beside you? Visual impact has a great appeal, especially with the colors, beautiful bright Red, nice clean White, good focus and sharpness on the strings and the screws and the rest of the hardware.The black background is also a nice choice. Im really sorry to see you score so low in this Challenge.  All I can say is to keep it simple the next time and get rid of the distracting objects, which catch the voters eyes.  Hope this helps!Hope to see more of your photographs next week, have fun at it,Regards,Ed', '2003-03-21 22:36:53.000000', NULL, 0),
(242968, 13612, 4388, '<td valign="top"><b><u>Critique Club critique:</u></b> <br/><b>Composition/Content:</b> <br/>The content is wonderful. It''s always nice to see soft white water flowing over rocks. Very nice composition with a foreground, middle ground and background. You would have scored higher if the challenge was nature or landscapes. What I mean by  that is the bridge should be the subject of your image.<br/><b>Camera Work/Technical:</b> <br/>Very nice camera angle and depth of field. Good exposure to soften the white water at the same time keeping a good tonal range.<br/><b>Post Processing:</b> <br/>I tried saturating the colors and it made the green in the water and the green in the rocks more prominant and tied the photo together with the greenery on the sides of the river. Cropping some of the sides out of the image also seems to help as if the camera zoomed in on the upper center of the image.<br/><b>My Opinion:</b> I think you created a beautiful photograph that could be a great photograph with saturating the color some and maybe cropping out some of the sides. If you want to score higher in the challenge, simplify your image and make sure the subject of your image is the main interest in the image. Great job.<br/></td>', 'Critique Club critique: Composition/Content: The content is wonderful. It''s always nice to see soft white water flowing over rocks. Very nice composition with a foreground, middle ground and background. You would have scored higher if the challenge was nature or landscapes. What I mean by  that is the bridge should be the subject of your image.Camera Work/Technical: Very nice camera angle and depth of field. Good exposure to soften the white water at the same time keeping a good tonal range.Post Processing: I tried saturating the colors and it made the green in the water and the green in the rocks more prominant and tied the photo together with the greenery on the sides of the river. Cropping some of the sides out of the image also seems to help as if the camera zoomed in on the upper center of the image.My Opinion: I think you created a beautiful photograph that could be a great photograph with saturating the color some and maybe cropping out some of the sides. If you want to score higher in the challenge, simplify your image and make sure the subject of your image is the main interest in the image. Great job.', '2003-03-21 23:02:36.000000', NULL, 0),
(242969, 13718, 1143, '<td valign="top">I wrote a really long critique that got lost somehow  *sigh* so here I am trying to remember it.<br/>First off. Always make sure that you meet the challenge because voters are very picky about that and will mark you way down. With this one, yes you have a bridge.<br/>Secondly, composition is very important. Simplicity is usually best. You want to show the subject without a lot of distractions. Using elements such as strong color or strong leading lines (perhaps a nice diagonal to lead the eye into the shot) or using the "rule of thirds" to place the subject... all of these are good to keep in mind when composing the picture. With this picture, the dominant color is brown (not usually very appealing but typical of the season). The bridge gets lost in all the branches. The eye doesn''t really know where to go. It also doesn''t help that the bridge goes directly across the middle of the picture. Showing the entire subject at a straight-on angle is usually not the best way to shoot. Also, if color isn''t really adding anything to the picture then you can always try it in black and white for a different effect. If you use photoshop, you can check out the different color channels to see how different it can look. Increasing the contrast with a black and white shot can also make it more dramatic and add a different look.<br/>The lighting in this picture isn''t the best. Shooting early in the morning or late in the day is the best. Middle afternoon shots are rarely as good.<br/>I don''t know if it is the lighting, but it looks oversharpened. Anything with a lot of small lines (like animal fur or these branches) will look oversharpened very easily. If you use PS, use the unsharp mask and a radius of 1. Then slowly raise the amount. Rarely will I go above 70% with any picture.<br/>For now, just learn to compose strong photos. Creativity is very important. Being able to show an ordinary subject in an appealing and interesting way is tricky.<br/>If you really wanted to use this bridge and didn''t have a better option then you would want to find a different, more appealing way to shoot it. Maybe a shot from on top... looking across to the other side. You could use repetition - the repeating boards along the railing with the top rail used as a diagonal leading into the shot. <br/>Look at the bridge pictures that you like and try to figure out whats appealing about them. Think about where the subject is placed, where the lines go, whether or not color is being used, etc. Eventually you''ll start thinking about all of that when you take a picture of your own.<br/>Good luck! Just take pictures that you like and don''t be too concerned with the score or comments. There will always be those who are better and those who are worse.</td>', 'I wrote a really long critique that got lost somehow  *sigh* so here I am trying to remember it.First off. Always make sure that you meet the challenge because voters are very picky about that and will mark you way down. With this one, yes you have a bridge.Secondly, composition is very important. Simplicity is usually best. You want to show the subject without a lot of distractions. Using elements such as strong color or strong leading lines (perhaps a nice diagonal to lead the eye into the shot) or using the "rule of thirds" to place the subject... all of these are good to keep in mind when composing the picture. With this picture, the dominant color is brown (not usually very appealing but typical of the season). The bridge gets lost in all the branches. The eye doesn''t really know where to go. It also doesn''t help that the bridge goes directly across the middle of the picture. Showing the entire subject at a straight-on angle is usually not the best way to shoot. Also, if color isn''t really adding anything to the picture then you can always try it in black and white for a different effect. If you use photoshop, you can check out the different color channels to see how different it can look. Increasing the contrast with a black and white shot can also make it more dramatic and add a different look.The lighting in this picture isn''t the best. Shooting early in the morning or late in the day is the best. Middle afternoon shots are rarely as good.I don''t know if it is the lighting, but it looks oversharpened. Anything with a lot of small lines (like animal fur or these branches) will look oversharpened very easily. If you use PS, use the unsharp mask and a radius of 1. Then slowly raise the amount. Rarely will I go above 70% with any picture.For now, just learn to compose strong photos. Creativity is very important. Being able to show an ordinary subject in an appealing and interesting way is tricky.If you really wanted to use this bridge and didn''t have a better option then you would want to find a different, more appealing way to shoot it. Maybe a shot from on top... looking across to the other side. You could use repetition - the repeating boards along the railing with the top rail used as a diagonal leading into the shot. Look at the bridge pictures that you like and try to figure out whats appealing about them. Think about where the subject is placed, where the lines go, whether or not color is being used, etc. Eventually you''ll start thinking about all of that when you take a picture of your own.Good luck! Just take pictures that you like and don''t be too concerned with the score or comments. There will always be those who are better and those who are worse.', '2003-03-21 23:03:22.000000', NULL, 0),
(242970, 13652, 6328, '<td valign="top">Golden Gate Bridge Underbelly<br/>Davidspyle<br/>Critique by Silver Fox<br/> <br/>Composition is splendid with a great depth of field.  <br/> <br/>The photograph does not really show a bridge per se, but in my humble opinion should still qualify for the Challenge.<br/> <br/>The visual impact of your photograph is very good due to the overall Focus and Sharpness of the architecture and color of the various patterns that we are able to see.  Youve captured great detail of a bridge that we would not normally get to see.  As I stop to really look at this photograph I suddenly saw that workman standing on the walkway halfway down the bridge.<br/> <br/>The colours of your photograph are really noticeable, from the reds to the oranges to the grays to whites.<br/> <br/>As I said before, the only thing I could find wrong was that your horizon was off by the tiniest bit but its pretty hard to carry a level around in our pocket when we go out to shoot photos.<br/> <br/>Dont worry about your score. This is only your first entry.  Get out, shoot some more, enter some more Challenges, have some fun, shoot some more, thats what life is all about.<br/> <br/>Have Fun!<br/>Ed<br/></td>', 'Golden Gate Bridge UnderbellyDavidspyleCritique by Silver Fox Composition is splendid with a great depth of field.   The photograph does not really show a bridge per se, but in my humble opinion should still qualify for the Challenge. The visual impact of your photograph is very good due to the overall Focus and Sharpness of the architecture and color of the various patterns that we are able to see.  Youve captured great detail of a bridge that we would not normally get to see.  As I stop to really look at this photograph I suddenly saw that workman standing on the walkway halfway down the bridge. The colours of your photograph are really noticeable, from the reds to the oranges to the grays to whites. As I said before, the only thing I could find wrong was that your horizon was off by the tiniest bit but its pretty hard to carry a level around in our pocket when we go out to shoot photos. Dont worry about your score. This is only your first entry.  Get out, shoot some more, enter some more Challenges, have some fun, shoot some more, thats what life is all about. Have Fun!Ed', '2003-03-21 23:06:58.000000', NULL, 0),
(242980, 13831, 3280, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings from the Critique Club };-) </b> <br/> <br/><b>Initial thoughts </b> <br/>Great bridge, meets the challenge, may be a bit dark.<br/> <br/><b>Composition/ Content </b> <br/>The composition is wonderful.  It makes me want to follow the bridge to see what is over that hill. The light bouncing off of the right of the bridge is excellent as well. <br/> <br/><b>Technical</b> <br/>Focus seems good and contrast is excellent for the difference in light and shadow you have here.  The only thing I would like to see is a little more light.  I think that would have brightened up the bridge and made the morning light even more of a focal point. It may have really made a killer shot.<br/> <br/><b>My Opinion On The Photo </b> <br/>I originally scored this shot a six. I think that it meets the challenge well but could have been improved a little to finish even higher than it did. Good job, and keep up the good work. <br/> <br/> <br/>I would be happy to talk further about this shot if you would like to contact me.<br/><b>DougPaz</b> <br/> <br/> <br/> <br/></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club };-)   Initial thoughts  Great bridge, meets the challenge, may be a bit dark. Composition/ Content  The composition is wonderful.  It makes me want to follow the bridge to see what is over that hill. The light bouncing off of the right of the bridge is excellent as well.  Technical Focus seems good and contrast is excellent for the difference in light and shadow you have here.  The only thing I would like to see is a little more light.  I think that would have brightened up the bridge and made the morning light even more of a focal point. It may have really made a killer shot. My Opinion On The Photo  I originally scored this shot a six. I think that it meets the challenge well but could have been improved a little to finish even higher than it did. Good job, and keep up the good work.   I would be happy to talk further about this shot if you would like to contact me.DougPaz    ', '2003-03-21 23:45:40.000000', NULL, 0),
(242985, 13816, 3280, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings from the Critique Club };-) </b> <br/> <br/><b>Initial thoughts </b> <br/>Beautiful bridge, meets the challenge, a little too much water in foreground.<br/> <br/><b>Composition/ Content </b> <br/>I really like this bridge a lot.  That being said, I wish I could see it a little better.  There is a bit too much water and not enough bridge.  I don''t know for sure if you have enough lens or there is a place to get close enough to it but a closer shot would be great. If not possible then you need to crop about half of the foreground water and make more of a panorama shot. <br/> <br/><b>Technical</b> <br/>Focus seems good but the contrast seems like it could be turned up just a bit to make the bridge stand out against the sky more. A blue sky would be great but it is awfully hard to control the weather! <br/> <br/><b>My Opinion On The Photo </b> <br/>I originally scored this shot a six which was a touch higher than most of the voters. I think that it meets the challenge well but could have been improved a little to finish even higher than it did. Good job, and keep up the good work. <br/> <br/> <br/>I would be happy to talk further about this shot if you would like to contact me.<br/><b>DougPaz</b> <br/></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club };-)   Initial thoughts  Beautiful bridge, meets the challenge, a little too much water in foreground. Composition/ Content  I really like this bridge a lot.  That being said, I wish I could see it a little better.  There is a bit too much water and not enough bridge.  I don''t know for sure if you have enough lens or there is a place to get close enough to it but a closer shot would be great. If not possible then you need to crop about half of the foreground water and make more of a panorama shot.  Technical Focus seems good but the contrast seems like it could be turned up just a bit to make the bridge stand out against the sky more. A blue sky would be great but it is awfully hard to control the weather!  My Opinion On The Photo  I originally scored this shot a six which was a touch higher than most of the voters. I think that it meets the challenge well but could have been improved a little to finish even higher than it did. Good job, and keep up the good work.   I would be happy to talk further about this shot if you would like to contact me.DougPaz ', '2003-03-21 23:53:44.000000', NULL, 0),
(242988, 13930, 3280, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings from the Critique Club };-) </b> <br/> <br/><b>Initial thoughts </b> <br/>Beautiful photo, barely meets the challenge, bridge is hidden.<br/> <br/><b>Composition/ Content </b> <br/>This is such a great shot but was wasted on this challenge as the bridge in it is hard to see and almost camoflaged. I love the water and the reflections of the buildings but the bridge is not the main focal point.<br/> <br/><b>Technical</b> <br/>Focus seems good.  The only thing I might have changed was to give it a touch more saturation so that blue sky would stand out.  Of course, I would have shot the bridge from a different angle to show it up more as well.<br/> <br/><b>My Opinion On The Photo </b> <br/>I originally scored this shot a four. I like it a lot but feel it is a stretch to say that it meets the challenge well.  I think it would have done nicely in a different challenge. Good job, and keep up the good work. <br/> <br/> <br/>I would be happy to talk further about this shot if you would like to contact me.<br/><b>DougPaz</b> <br/></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club };-)   Initial thoughts  Beautiful photo, barely meets the challenge, bridge is hidden. Composition/ Content  This is such a great shot but was wasted on this challenge as the bridge in it is hard to see and almost camoflaged. I love the water and the reflections of the buildings but the bridge is not the main focal point. Technical Focus seems good.  The only thing I might have changed was to give it a touch more saturation so that blue sky would stand out.  Of course, I would have shot the bridge from a different angle to show it up more as well. My Opinion On The Photo  I originally scored this shot a four. I like it a lot but feel it is a stretch to say that it meets the challenge well.  I think it would have done nicely in a different challenge. Good job, and keep up the good work.   I would be happy to talk further about this shot if you would like to contact me.DougPaz ', '2003-03-21 23:59:51.000000', NULL, 0),
(242994, 13770, 3280, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings from the Critique Club };-) </b> <br/> <br/><b>Initial thoughts </b> <br/>Beautiful composition,  definitely meets the challenge, nice color.<br/> <br/><b>Composition/ Content </b> <br/>I really enjoyed this shot but I''m a sucker for framing anyway. A great eye as you''ve shown many times in the past to capture this one. I like the color as it really adds to the mistiness of the shot. The only thing I might have changed here is to crop off a bit more from the left.  I think that the repetition of the bridge columns takes focus away from the framed bridge which should be your focal point.  I would crop it right down the middle of those columns.<br/> <br/><b>Technical</b> <br/>Very nice focus under difficult conditions. Your contrast is right on, once again under tough exposure. <br/> <br/><b>My Opinion On The Photo </b> <br/>I originally scored this shot a seven. I think that it meets the challenge well but could have been improved a little to finish even higher than it did. Great job, and keep up the good work. <br/> <br/> <br/>I would be happy to talk further about this shot if you would like to contact me.<br/><b>DougPaz</b> <br/></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club };-)   Initial thoughts  Beautiful composition,  definitely meets the challenge, nice color. Composition/ Content  I really enjoyed this shot but I''m a sucker for framing anyway. A great eye as you''ve shown many times in the past to capture this one. I like the color as it really adds to the mistiness of the shot. The only thing I might have changed here is to crop off a bit more from the left.  I think that the repetition of the bridge columns takes focus away from the framed bridge which should be your focal point.  I would crop it right down the middle of those columns. Technical Very nice focus under difficult conditions. Your contrast is right on, once again under tough exposure.  My Opinion On The Photo  I originally scored this shot a seven. I think that it meets the challenge well but could have been improved a little to finish even higher than it did. Great job, and keep up the good work.   I would be happy to talk further about this shot if you would like to contact me.DougPaz ', '2003-03-22 00:19:49.000000', NULL, 0),
(243021, 13591, 6172, '<td valign="top">This is very nice work, I''m dissapointed at what it was given for a Final Score, I think you deserved higher.  Nice job with the sepia, it is so appropriate in this shot.  Even the background buildings fit well with your attempt to make this look old.  I just have two suggestions that you can take or leave for what they are worth.  1.) Maybe a slightly different angle would have been better as the two houses on the ends of the bridge appear to be growing out of the bridge.  2.) Mabye placing the bridge higher in the photo (at 2/3 instead of 1/2)would have been more appropriate.  The buildings in the background would have been still visible, but there would have been less sky and the bridge would have been more prominent.  Keep up the good work!</td>', 'This is very nice work, I''m dissapointed at what it was given for a Final Score, I think you deserved higher.  Nice job with the sepia, it is so appropriate in this shot.  Even the background buildings fit well with your attempt to make this look old.  I just have two suggestions that you can take or leave for what they are worth.  1.) Maybe a slightly different angle would have been better as the two houses on the ends of the bridge appear to be growing out of the bridge.  2.) Mabye placing the bridge higher in the photo (at 2/3 instead of 1/2)would have been more appropriate.  The buildings in the background would have been still visible, but there would have been less sky and the bridge would have been more prominent.  Keep up the good work!', '2003-03-22 02:36:57.000000', NULL, 0),
(243228, 13657, 6328, '<td valign="top">Deserted bridge<br/>By mayas<br/>Critique by Silver Fox<br/> <br/>Composition photo of the bridge is pretty good as is the depth of field.  <br/>It fit the Challenge just fine.<br/> <br/>Your photograph is very busy with the walls of the cliffs surrounding the bridge and the trees overhanging it with all the tree limbs.  It would perhaps have been better if you could have taken the shot a bit lower down to try and get away from so many distractions to take a look at the bridge from the other direction.<br/> <br/>The visual interest of the bridge is brought alive by the whiteness and the strong lighting coming from above the tree levels.<br/>Your focus is strong on the tree line in front but you start to lose the sharpness as one starts to look towards the bridge itself.<br/> <br/>Your photograph shows good concept and inspiration and is really interesting to look at.<br/> <br/>This is only your second piece that youve entered into the DP Challenge and I would encourage you to shoot more images and have some fun and enter more challenges in the coming weeks and months.  I look forward to seeing more of your work.<br/> <br/>Sincerely,<br/>Ed<br/> <br/></td>', 'Deserted bridgeBy mayasCritique by Silver Fox Composition photo of the bridge is pretty good as is the depth of field.  It fit the Challenge just fine. Your photograph is very busy with the walls of the cliffs surrounding the bridge and the trees overhanging it with all the tree limbs.  It would perhaps have been better if you could have taken the shot a bit lower down to try and get away from so many distractions to take a look at the bridge from the other direction. The visual interest of the bridge is brought alive by the whiteness and the strong lighting coming from above the tree levels.Your focus is strong on the tree line in front but you start to lose the sharpness as one starts to look towards the bridge itself. Your photograph shows good concept and inspiration and is really interesting to look at. This is only your second piece that youve entered into the DP Challenge and I would encourage you to shoot more images and have some fun and enter more challenges in the coming weeks and months.  I look forward to seeing more of your work. Sincerely,Ed ', '2003-03-22 20:35:52.000000', NULL, 0),
(243233, 13668, 811, '<td valign="top">CRITIQUE CLUB CRITIQUE<br/>by karmat<br/> <br/>COMPOSITION<br/>You have chosen what I think is a potentially captivating picture, but I think a couple of things could have been done with the composition to make it a little more engaging and/or dynamic.  It feels off-balanced to me.  Though the pipe thing leads into the picture well, I think it is a little too vertical.  Maybe if you could have made it so that it was leading more towards the center of the picture instead of almost straight up the left edge, it would have been better.  Overall, I think if you could have gotten closer to the bridge, it would have had more immediate appeal, as well.<br/> <br/>TECHNIQUE<br/>Your focus and exposure is great as many of the details are visible throughout the picture.  The bridge does seem to blend into the sky, somewhat.  Too bad it wasn''t a clear day, huh?  Something that might help is to desaturate all the colors except blue.  This would minimize the blandness of the sky, and keep that interesting blue streak across the bridge.<br/> <br/>OVERALL EFFECT<br/>It is a good picture, technically, and the blue streak across the bridge really grabbed my attention.  I think changing the angle a bit, getting closer might make it a little more striking.<br/> <br/>Best to you in the future challenges. <br/>karmat<br/></td>', 'CRITIQUE CLUB CRITIQUEby karmat COMPOSITIONYou have chosen what I think is a potentially captivating picture, but I think a couple of things could have been done with the composition to make it a little more engaging and/or dynamic.  It feels off-balanced to me.  Though the pipe thing leads into the picture well, I think it is a little too vertical.  Maybe if you could have made it so that it was leading more towards the center of the picture instead of almost straight up the left edge, it would have been better.  Overall, I think if you could have gotten closer to the bridge, it would have had more immediate appeal, as well. TECHNIQUEYour focus and exposure is great as many of the details are visible throughout the picture.  The bridge does seem to blend into the sky, somewhat.  Too bad it wasn''t a clear day, huh?  Something that might help is to desaturate all the colors except blue.  This would minimize the blandness of the sky, and keep that interesting blue streak across the bridge. OVERALL EFFECTIt is a good picture, technically, and the blue streak across the bridge really grabbed my attention.  I think changing the angle a bit, getting closer might make it a little more striking. Best to you in the future challenges. karmat', '2003-03-22 20:47:12.000000', NULL, 0),
(243247, 13539, 6328, '<td valign="top">Bridge perspective<br/>By rogerspaul<br/>Critique by Silver Fox<br/> <br/>I think the composition is fine and the photograph fit the Challenge okay.<br/> <br/>It was a long depth of field with the length of the bridge and gives a good visual appeal with your dog prancing towards you like hes doing.<br/> <br/>You got a low angle in your photo that gives a different perspective to your photograph and shows that you have some imagination and creativeness in you.<br/> <br/>The problem is that you seem to be out of focus on the decking and on the dog, what happened here?  See the branches on the left?  Perhaps moving in on your subject (dog) a little more would have taken them out of the photo altogether and allowed you to focus more on your dog.<br/> <br/>Overall, youve got the makings of a good photograph but your finishing score of 4.8 tells you that you made some mistakes.  <br/> <br/>Correct them, go out, take more photos, clean them up, enter more Challenges, have yourself some fun, and lets watch your score move up higher.<br/> <br/>Regards,<br/>Ed<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2003-03-22 21:26:43.</i></td>', 'Bridge perspectiveBy rogerspaulCritique by Silver Fox I think the composition is fine and the photograph fit the Challenge okay. It was a long depth of field with the length of the bridge and gives a good visual appeal with your dog prancing towards you like hes doing. You got a low angle in your photo that gives a different perspective to your photograph and shows that you have some imagination and creativeness in you. The problem is that you seem to be out of focus on the decking and on the dog, what happened here?  See the branches on the left?  Perhaps moving in on your subject (dog) a little more would have taken them out of the photo altogether and allowed you to focus more on your dog. Overall, youve got the makings of a good photograph but your finishing score of 4.8 tells you that you made some mistakes.   Correct them, go out, take more photos, clean them up, enter more Challenges, have yourself some fun, and lets watch your score move up higher. Regards,Ed', '2003-03-22 21:23:58.000000', '2003-03-22 21:26:43.000000', 0),
(243381, 13748, 3599, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club.<br/> <br/>Congratulations on a very good photo.  This was certainly one of the most creative shots for the challenge.  There isn''t much I can say to add to this photo.  I really like the purplish hue in the photo it does give it a nice soft look.  Sorry that I can''t add more but the photo is very nice and I can''t see any other way doing it.  Looking forward to seeing your stuff in future challenges.</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club. Congratulations on a very good photo.  This was certainly one of the most creative shots for the challenge.  There isn''t much I can say to add to this photo.  I really like the purplish hue in the photo it does give it a nice soft look.  Sorry that I can''t add more but the photo is very nice and I can''t see any other way doing it.  Looking forward to seeing your stuff in future challenges.', '2003-03-23 06:02:17.000000', NULL, 0),
(243441, 13803, 811, '<td valign="top">CRITIQUE CLUB CRITIQUE<br/>by karmat<br/> <br/>COMPOSITION<br/>I like how you have taken a picture of something "ordinary," or at least common, and used made it interesting by making it into an abstract.  It is composed really well, I think, because the eye follows the ramp down the right and across the frame at the bottom, then loops back.  excellent.  Did you try flipping so that it would be a left to right flow?  I don''t know that it would have been necessarily better, just different.<br/>The sky also produces negative space which is very effective in this shot to emphasize the bridges.<br/> <br/>TECHNIQUE<br/>The blue sky really makes this shot, though it almost seems a touch blown out at the bottom.  Not enough to really detract though.  I, personally, do not see the artifacts others are mentioning, but then again, maybe I need glasses or something.  To me, it looks focused and clear.  I like the way hte shadows help accentuate the motion.  I do see some the moire pattern or banding in the sky.  Perhaps NEATIMAGE, or another similar filter could help smooth that out<br/> <br/>OVERALL EFFECT<br/>This shot seems to serve to show the vastness of the road system.  Though it is but a small part, and is a simple picture, it speaks of the massive confusion that sometimes comes when trying to navigate such things (especially for me!)<br/> <br/>Good work and best to you in future challenges</td>', 'CRITIQUE CLUB CRITIQUEby karmat COMPOSITIONI like how you have taken a picture of something "ordinary," or at least common, and used made it interesting by making it into an abstract.  It is composed really well, I think, because the eye follows the ramp down the right and across the frame at the bottom, then loops back.  excellent.  Did you try flipping so that it would be a left to right flow?  I don''t know that it would have been necessarily better, just different.The sky also produces negative space which is very effective in this shot to emphasize the bridges. TECHNIQUEThe blue sky really makes this shot, though it almost seems a touch blown out at the bottom.  Not enough to really detract though.  I, personally, do not see the artifacts others are mentioning, but then again, maybe I need glasses or something.  To me, it looks focused and clear.  I like the way hte shadows help accentuate the motion.  I do see some the moire pattern or banding in the sky.  Perhaps NEATIMAGE, or another similar filter could help smooth that out OVERALL EFFECTThis shot seems to serve to show the vastness of the road system.  Though it is but a small part, and is a simple picture, it speaks of the massive confusion that sometimes comes when trying to navigate such things (especially for me!) Good work and best to you in future challenges', '2003-03-23 11:19:39.000000', NULL, 0),
(243460, 13794, 4005, '<td valign="top">Köln!<br/>Schööön... ein umwerfender Anblick!<br/>Gratuliere zu dem traumhaften bild... bin ein bischen neidisch - wär auch gern mal wieder da!<br/>Liebe grüße und wie gesagt - tolles bild, gut getroffen... ich mag es wenn die lichter zu sternen werden und es trifft sich natürlich gut, daß auch noch grad ein zug durchfährt...<br/> <br/>Martina.</td>', 'Köln!Schööön... ein umwerfender Anblick!Gratuliere zu dem traumhaften bild... bin ein bischen neidisch - wär auch gern mal wieder da!Liebe grüße und wie gesagt - tolles bild, gut getroffen... ich mag es wenn die lichter zu sternen werden und es trifft sich natürlich gut, daß auch noch grad ein zug durchfährt... Martina.', '2003-03-23 12:37:43.000000', NULL, 0),
(243569, 13841, 527, '<td valign="top">Critique Club critique by Tim Jensen.<br/> <br/>This is a very interesting bridge and I love the way the snow is piled up on the sides of the columns. I am not completely in favor of the composition, however. It''s not bad but I think you could have chosen a better angle that eliminated the branches and was more dramatic. I''m not sure exactly what that angle should be but maybe one that highlighted one of the piles of snow and an arch by showing it closer to the camera. Basically looking down the side of the bridges some more. Since I can''t see the entire scene I can''t know if there were some elements that you didn''t want to show in the scene. The repetition of the arches and piles of snow are very interesting and I would like to see them pronounced even more in some way. The same goes for the nice texture of the bridge. I just want to get closer to all of that.<br/> <br/>The colors and values are very good and natural looking. I really like the smoothness and brightness of the snow and the way that it helps lead you into the photo. Within the second arch from the right there are trees that are interfering from clearing showing the arche''s shape on the backside. The arches are an important visual element and care should be taken so that they are not blocked or interfered with.<br/> <br/>The photo is appropriately sharp and executed very well. Overall this is a pleasing photo of a very intersting bridge and surrounding elements. Good job<br/> <br/>Tim</td>', 'Critique Club critique by Tim Jensen. This is a very interesting bridge and I love the way the snow is piled up on the sides of the columns. I am not completely in favor of the composition, however. It''s not bad but I think you could have chosen a better angle that eliminated the branches and was more dramatic. I''m not sure exactly what that angle should be but maybe one that highlighted one of the piles of snow and an arch by showing it closer to the camera. Basically looking down the side of the bridges some more. Since I can''t see the entire scene I can''t know if there were some elements that you didn''t want to show in the scene. The repetition of the arches and piles of snow are very interesting and I would like to see them pronounced even more in some way. The same goes for the nice texture of the bridge. I just want to get closer to all of that. The colors and values are very good and natural looking. I really like the smoothness and brightness of the snow and the way that it helps lead you into the photo. Within the second arch from the right there are trees that are interfering from clearing showing the arche''s shape on the backside. The arches are an important visual element and care should be taken so that they are not blocked or interfered with. The photo is appropriately sharp and executed very well. Overall this is a pleasing photo of a very intersting bridge and surrounding elements. Good job Tim', '2003-03-23 16:31:12.000000', NULL, 0),
(243583, 13762, 527, '<td valign="top">Critique Club critique by Tim Jensen.<br/> <br/>The first thing that caught my attention was the strong visual impact of this photo due to its dynamic composition. Compositionally it is very effective in communicating the sense of beauty, size, and strength of this structure. I would have preferred to not see the trees just to the right of the closest column. Instead, by moving to the right you could have blocked those trees with the column and displayed a solid blue shape that would better reflect the blue area on the left side of the bridge. I understand why the image is tilted slightly so that the apparent horizon line is level but by doing that the columns are tilted and the bridges looses some of its structural balance. it''s a bit of a trade off and I am simply wondering what it would look and feel like with the columns more vertical.<br/> <br/>Your intent was to shoot this scene at mid day and while that works just fine I am wondering what this would look like taken at dusk or nighttime with a much darker background. A timed exposure could give a real interesting deep blue color that could compliment the color of the concrete very well. It could simplify much of the background and empahasize the composition even more. Of course I would have to see that to know for sure. Just an idea.<br/> <br/>The colors and values are very natural looking and the contrast is appropriately bold. It is very sharp but I think I would have preferred a much shallower depth of field where the focus was on the foreground 1/3 of the bridge and to go slightly soft as it receded. I feel that this would support the already strong sense of depth and 3dimensionality (is that a word?).<br/> <br/>I love how you took a pretty common structure and created a very artistic image out of it. Good job.<br/> <br/>Tim</td>', 'Critique Club critique by Tim Jensen. The first thing that caught my attention was the strong visual impact of this photo due to its dynamic composition. Compositionally it is very effective in communicating the sense of beauty, size, and strength of this structure. I would have preferred to not see the trees just to the right of the closest column. Instead, by moving to the right you could have blocked those trees with the column and displayed a solid blue shape that would better reflect the blue area on the left side of the bridge. I understand why the image is tilted slightly so that the apparent horizon line is level but by doing that the columns are tilted and the bridges looses some of its structural balance. it''s a bit of a trade off and I am simply wondering what it would look and feel like with the columns more vertical. Your intent was to shoot this scene at mid day and while that works just fine I am wondering what this would look like taken at dusk or nighttime with a much darker background. A timed exposure could give a real interesting deep blue color that could compliment the color of the concrete very well. It could simplify much of the background and empahasize the composition even more. Of course I would have to see that to know for sure. Just an idea. The colors and values are very natural looking and the contrast is appropriately bold. It is very sharp but I think I would have preferred a much shallower depth of field where the focus was on the foreground 1/3 of the bridge and to go slightly soft as it receded. I feel that this would support the already strong sense of depth and 3dimensionality (is that a word?). I love how you took a pretty common structure and created a very artistic image out of it. Good job. Tim', '2003-03-23 17:07:54.000000', NULL, 0),
(243588, 13554, 811, '<td valign="top">CRITIQUE CLUB CRITIQUE<br/>by karmat<br/> <br/>COMPOSITION<br/>A very interesting composition.  I think it is good how you have the tracks, poles, adn wires leading into the upper left corner of the frame, while the landscape provides contrast and leads the eyes towards the right of the frame. The empty space on the right is a little distracting in that it is not truly negative space, because there is still "stuff" there, so it doesn''t point entirely to the tracks and train.  Perhaps a vertical cropping eliminating the right side and showing more of the top of the train would have added impact.<br/> <br/>TECHNIQUE<br/>The stark bw of this shot is effective at giving an "empty" feeling to the landscape.  It almost looks desolate.  I like how the mountains in the background fade into what looks like fog or mist and it seems very sharp to me.  <br/> <br/>OVERALL EFFECT<br/>Again, I think your coloring, framing, etc. combine to give this pic a less than hopeful feeling.  It is a very good picture in that it is technically well done, adn evokes emotion.  <br/> <br/>Best to you in future challenges.<br/>karmat<br/></td>', 'CRITIQUE CLUB CRITIQUEby karmat COMPOSITIONA very interesting composition.  I think it is good how you have the tracks, poles, adn wires leading into the upper left corner of the frame, while the landscape provides contrast and leads the eyes towards the right of the frame. The empty space on the right is a little distracting in that it is not truly negative space, because there is still "stuff" there, so it doesn''t point entirely to the tracks and train.  Perhaps a vertical cropping eliminating the right side and showing more of the top of the train would have added impact. TECHNIQUEThe stark bw of this shot is effective at giving an "empty" feeling to the landscape.  It almost looks desolate.  I like how the mountains in the background fade into what looks like fog or mist and it seems very sharp to me.   OVERALL EFFECTAgain, I think your coloring, framing, etc. combine to give this pic a less than hopeful feeling.  It is a very good picture in that it is technically well done, adn evokes emotion.   Best to you in future challenges.karmat', '2003-03-23 17:12:20.000000', NULL, 0),
(243597, 13873, 527, '<td valign="top">An average of 6.7 from the non camera users? A full point lower then the others. Unbelievable. End of tiny rant.<br/> <br/>This photo is awesome. This deserves to be displayed large. I think you should definitely try to promote this image because everybody will love this and you could make some good money off of it.<br/> <br/>The choice of colors is perfect as well as the composition and detail. <br/> <br/>I guess you can tell how much I love this photo. Great job!<br/> <br/>T</td>', 'An average of 6.7 from the non camera users? A full point lower then the others. Unbelievable. End of tiny rant. This photo is awesome. This deserves to be displayed large. I think you should definitely try to promote this image because everybody will love this and you could make some good money off of it. The choice of colors is perfect as well as the composition and detail.  I guess you can tell how much I love this photo. Great job! T', '2003-03-23 17:18:07.000000', NULL, 0),
(243714, 13974, 1280, '<td valign="top">Critique Club Comments by Grayce<br/>Congratulations on a beautiful image!  I like this very much because of the techniques you employed to create it.  The time you put into it paid off.<br/>The soft focus works with the sepia to give an aged, nostalgic mood to it.<br/>Your composition is good with fine perspective, plus just the right amount of foreground and background to give a sense of place.<br/>Exposure is a tad dark, but I think it''s fine. Contrast is soft and I like it that way, for this image.<br/>Post processing is fine, with no digital noise.<br/>Overall this has an ethereal quality and qualifies as art.  Very nice job!<br/>Regards,<br/> <br/>Grayce <br/></td>', 'Critique Club Comments by GrayceCongratulations on a beautiful image!  I like this very much because of the techniques you employed to create it.  The time you put into it paid off.The soft focus works with the sepia to give an aged, nostalgic mood to it.Your composition is good with fine perspective, plus just the right amount of foreground and background to give a sense of place.Exposure is a tad dark, but I think it''s fine. Contrast is soft and I like it that way, for this image.Post processing is fine, with no digital noise.Overall this has an ethereal quality and qualifies as art.  Very nice job!Regards, Grayce ', '2003-03-23 20:34:38.000000', NULL, 0),
(243718, 13650, 4162, '<td valign="top">Critique Club<br/> <br/>Composition-Content<br/>The angle that you have shot this bridge is great, with the lines from the top right corner, leading the viewer across the bridge and into the picture. The balance between the blue sky and slightly bluish snow is also great.<br/> <br/>Technique<br/>Lighting is very beautiful here, with the light on the arches, which brings the viewers attention to the arches and lovely brickwork. I agree with some of the comments, that if there was a little more light under the bridge, it would have brought out an interesting perspective and added more depth to the shot. Perhaps a tiny bit later would have added this lower lighting.<br/> <br/>My Opinion<br/>You found a lovely bridge and made the most of it with the composition and lighting. Great job and congrats on a very good score!</td>', 'Critique Club Composition-ContentThe angle that you have shot this bridge is great, with the lines from the top right corner, leading the viewer across the bridge and into the picture. The balance between the blue sky and slightly bluish snow is also great. TechniqueLighting is very beautiful here, with the light on the arches, which brings the viewers attention to the arches and lovely brickwork. I agree with some of the comments, that if there was a little more light under the bridge, it would have brought out an interesting perspective and added more depth to the shot. Perhaps a tiny bit later would have added this lower lighting. My OpinionYou found a lovely bridge and made the most of it with the composition and lighting. Great job and congrats on a very good score!', '2003-03-23 20:58:14.000000', NULL, 0),
(243753, 13750, 4162, '<td valign="top">Critique Club<br/> <br/>Composition-Content<br/> <br/>You found a lovely bridge with a lot of character, which makes a very interesting picture. I feel that the white building is slightly hich in the shot, perhaps cropping off abit of the water, then bringing the white building would bring more of a relaxed and peaceful feel to the shot. The house on the right adds dimension to the shot, but I wonder if the picture would have been nicer without it, I think that it isn''t necessary in the shot, as the bridge and white building add enough interest.  You have framed the picture very nicely with the trees.<br/> <br/>Background<br/> <br/>People mentioned the white sky, I am from England, I understand!! There really isn''t much you can do about it within a time frame, but you have done well in cropping out much of the sky and filling it with the landscape.<br/> <br/>My Opinion<br/> <br/>Lovely shot, very interesting bridge. Perhaps a slightly smaller border would have been good and complemented the scene more. Good Luck in the next challenge.</td>', 'Critique Club Composition-Content You found a lovely bridge with a lot of character, which makes a very interesting picture. I feel that the white building is slightly hich in the shot, perhaps cropping off abit of the water, then bringing the white building would bring more of a relaxed and peaceful feel to the shot. The house on the right adds dimension to the shot, but I wonder if the picture would have been nicer without it, I think that it isn''t necessary in the shot, as the bridge and white building add enough interest.  You have framed the picture very nicely with the trees. Background People mentioned the white sky, I am from England, I understand!! There really isn''t much you can do about it within a time frame, but you have done well in cropping out much of the sky and filling it with the landscape. My Opinion Lovely shot, very interesting bridge. Perhaps a slightly smaller border would have been good and complemented the scene more. Good Luck in the next challenge.', '2003-03-23 21:19:22.000000', NULL, 0),
(243786, 13561, 2221, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club, David :)<br/> <br/>Composition: Good. I like the angle you used. There is some wasted space in the upper right corner. It''s mostly black and the strings seem to be some kind of visual "barrier" which keep your eyes in the lower left corner, where the light is. There are two brighter spots under the strings in the black area, but I can''t really see what they are (it''s not the water). That''s a bit distracting. Either add more light to the black area or remove these spots. I also would like to see the full shape of this backward "S" formed hole (unfortunately I don''t know the correct English term).<br/> <br/>Lighting: Like others in the comments said: Gorgeous. I like the mix of light and shadow very much. Good job!<br/> <br/>Focus: The focus is good for my taste. The narrow aperture creates a sufficient DOF.<br/> <br/>Challenge: In my opinion adding the water was not necessary. But I understand the play of words you wanted to achieve. Anyway, personally I think it''s a bit too much "staged" and a more natural approach to the nice forms of the cello would be better.<br/> <br/>Creativity: A unique take on the challenge! Good idea!<br/></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club, David :) Composition: Good. I like the angle you used. There is some wasted space in the upper right corner. It''s mostly black and the strings seem to be some kind of visual "barrier" which keep your eyes in the lower left corner, where the light is. There are two brighter spots under the strings in the black area, but I can''t really see what they are (it''s not the water). That''s a bit distracting. Either add more light to the black area or remove these spots. I also would like to see the full shape of this backward "S" formed hole (unfortunately I don''t know the correct English term). Lighting: Like others in the comments said: Gorgeous. I like the mix of light and shadow very much. Good job! Focus: The focus is good for my taste. The narrow aperture creates a sufficient DOF. Challenge: In my opinion adding the water was not necessary. But I understand the play of words you wanted to achieve. Anyway, personally I think it''s a bit too much "staged" and a more natural approach to the nice forms of the cello would be better. Creativity: A unique take on the challenge! Good idea!', '2003-03-23 21:41:17.000000', NULL, 0),
(243792, 13796, 4162, '<td valign="top">Critique Club<br/> <br/>Composition-Content<br/> <br/>Composition is great, with the two bridges mirroring each other. I think that it is a very strong architectural shot, with the details of the bridges showing very well.<br/> <br/>Technical<br/> <br/>Personally, I really like the effect, it is strong, original and has impact. Unfortunately, I think that a lot of voters on this site prefer the more "traditional" approach, which is where your score suffered. I agree that this is a love it or hate it effect. I love it and the process you used is interesting and something which would be fun to play around with in the future.  Focus is spot on and Dof is great, even though your aperture is only 3.5. Just wondering why you chose to use such a fast shutter speed and large aperture. The end result is great with good sharpness throughout the image, which I am kind of surprised about! <br/> <br/>My Opinion<br/>Great shot, original and has impact! Shame that the score didn''t quite get over 5! However, I am sure that you are happy with the result of the shot, it''s just not everyones taste! Personally, I love it!</td>', 'Critique Club Composition-Content Composition is great, with the two bridges mirroring each other. I think that it is a very strong architectural shot, with the details of the bridges showing very well. Technical Personally, I really like the effect, it is strong, original and has impact. Unfortunately, I think that a lot of voters on this site prefer the more "traditional" approach, which is where your score suffered. I agree that this is a love it or hate it effect. I love it and the process you used is interesting and something which would be fun to play around with in the future.  Focus is spot on and Dof is great, even though your aperture is only 3.5. Just wondering why you chose to use such a fast shutter speed and large aperture. The end result is great with good sharpness throughout the image, which I am kind of surprised about!  My OpinionGreat shot, original and has impact! Shame that the score didn''t quite get over 5! However, I am sure that you are happy with the result of the shot, it''s just not everyones taste! Personally, I love it!', '2003-03-23 21:45:45.000000', NULL, 0),
(243863, 13779, 3280, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings from the Critique Club };-) </b> <br/> <br/><b>Initial thoughts </b> <br/>A real stretch for the challenge, not your best work technically.<br/> <br/><b>Composition/ Content </b> <br/>OK, you took a chance with the name.  I''m sure you got a few laughs and didn''t really care about the score so it worked.  I felt it was a bit of a stretch for the challenge as apparently did the voters.  The tower seems to be leaning to the right but not enough to validate the title.  If you had more of an angle, it might have worked a bit better.<br/> <br/> <br/><b>Technical</b> <br/>Focus seems a little soft and seems like it could use some sharpening.  The whole shot feels unbalanced.  I think I would have cropped it above the fence so that the legs of the tower would lead you to the Name on the tower.  As it is, the name seems lost in the shot. It seems a little washed out as well, perhaps and little more contrast and some saturation in post processing may have helped here.<br/> <br/><b>My Opinion On The Photo </b> <br/>I think you were probably just having some fun and it works that way. I think you could have improved it technically and still had a much better score.  There are great technical shots that are way off the challenge that score pretty well. Keep on shooting!!<br/> <br/> <br/>I would be happy to talk further about this shot if you would like to contact me.<br/><b>DougPaz</b> <br/></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club };-)   Initial thoughts  A real stretch for the challenge, not your best work technically. Composition/ Content  OK, you took a chance with the name.  I''m sure you got a few laughs and didn''t really care about the score so it worked.  I felt it was a bit of a stretch for the challenge as apparently did the voters.  The tower seems to be leaning to the right but not enough to validate the title.  If you had more of an angle, it might have worked a bit better.  Technical Focus seems a little soft and seems like it could use some sharpening.  The whole shot feels unbalanced.  I think I would have cropped it above the fence so that the legs of the tower would lead you to the Name on the tower.  As it is, the name seems lost in the shot. It seems a little washed out as well, perhaps and little more contrast and some saturation in post processing may have helped here. My Opinion On The Photo  I think you were probably just having some fun and it works that way. I think you could have improved it technically and still had a much better score.  There are great technical shots that are way off the challenge that score pretty well. Keep on shooting!!  I would be happy to talk further about this shot if you would like to contact me.DougPaz ', '2003-03-23 23:07:44.000000', NULL, 0),
(243934, 13740, 811, '<td valign="top">Joshua,<br/>I apologize to you for not getting a critique club critique done on this picture.  We had a computer crash that kept me offline for a while.<br/> <br/>Anyway, this is an awesome shot.  You have captured the sun rays very well (something I still cannot do!) and you managed to get the landscape well without blowing out the sky.  It may be a touch dark, but I can clearly see the bridge on my monitor.  I like the colors and the reflections in the water.  The way the bridge and shoreline converge makes for nice leading lines as well.  This is a very good shot, and I''m sorry it didn''t score higher.</td>', 'Joshua,I apologize to you for not getting a critique club critique done on this picture.  We had a computer crash that kept me offline for a while. Anyway, this is an awesome shot.  You have captured the sun rays very well (something I still cannot do!) and you managed to get the landscape well without blowing out the sky.  It may be a touch dark, but I can clearly see the bridge on my monitor.  I like the colors and the reflections in the water.  The way the bridge and shoreline converge makes for nice leading lines as well.  This is a very good shot, and I''m sorry it didn''t score higher.', '2003-03-24 00:18:10.000000', NULL, 0),
(244432, 13762, 1202, '<td valign="top">Tim,<br/>Thanks for a very helpful critique on my " No Turning On Bridge".   Each of your ideas sound good to me and I certainly appreciate your input.   This was a situation where I was rushed to get a shot and of the several I had, I had to choose one to go with.   My vantage point didn''t allow much movement and it seemed as though something else would always jump into the photo.  Keep up the great work on the CC, I and the rest of the DPC''rs thank you all.<br/>Dick Pattee</td>', 'Tim,Thanks for a very helpful critique on my " No Turning On Bridge".   Each of your ideas sound good to me and I certainly appreciate your input.   This was a situation where I was rushed to get a shot and of the several I had, I had to choose one to go with.   My vantage point didn''t allow much movement and it seemed as though something else would always jump into the photo.  Keep up the great work on the CC, I and the rest of the DPC''rs thank you all.Dick Pattee', '2003-03-24 10:36:13.000000', NULL, 0),
(246369, 13864, 1647, '<td valign="top">I love the colours - the blue in the background and the orange in the foreground.  They are so vivid and strong.<br/> <br/>Personally, I might have included a little more of the foreground in the shot - probably just by tilting the camera down slightly.  I might have also tried framing the shot with the fence posts in shot but the gate out of shot to see how it looked.<br/> <br/>I like the aspect ratio - I''m a sucker for a nice panoramic at the best of times.<br/> <br/>Finally the focus is pretty good but some of the foreground is slightly blurred.  Did you use infinity focus?  Did you use a tripod?  I suspect yes, but if not that they might help you.</td>', 'I love the colours - the blue in the background and the orange in the foreground.  They are so vivid and strong. Personally, I might have included a little more of the foreground in the shot - probably just by tilting the camera down slightly.  I might have also tried framing the shot with the fence posts in shot but the gate out of shot to see how it looked. I like the aspect ratio - I''m a sucker for a nice panoramic at the best of times. Finally the focus is pretty good but some of the foreground is slightly blurred.  Did you use infinity focus?  Did you use a tripod?  I suspect yes, but if not that they might help you.', '2003-03-25 22:26:16.000000', NULL, 0),
(247808, 13727, 7160, '<td valign="top">That bright white spot at the bottom of the photo is just a patch of sunlight--it was a beautiful day when I took the picture!  I wanted to crop it out, but I had already cropped the photo quite a bit and if I had completely removed the spot I would also have removed one of the ducks!  This was kind of a "spur of the moment" shot so I hope to do much better in the future.  Thanks to everyone who made comments!</td>', 'That bright white spot at the bottom of the photo is just a patch of sunlight--it was a beautiful day when I took the picture!  I wanted to crop it out, but I had already cropped the photo quite a bit and if I had completely removed the spot I would also have removed one of the ducks!  This was kind of a "spur of the moment" shot so I hope to do much better in the future.  Thanks to everyone who made comments!', '2003-03-28 11:30:35.000000', NULL, 0),
